**Solution Explanation**

For every unit of depth Percy loses one unit of oxygen.
At the beginning he is at depth `0` with `O` oxygen.
There are `N` stations

```
depth i   (different for every station)
time  i   – the time that must be spent at the station
```

If Percy reaches a station he may refill the tank **once** –  
the tank gains exactly `C` units of oxygen and he has to pay the
corresponding time.  
The goal is to reach the cave at depth `F` without the oxygen level
ever becoming negative and, among all possible ways, minimise the
total time spent at stations.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.  Observations

* The stations are only useful when they lie on the way from the
  surface to the cave.  
  While descending we always encounter the stations in increasing
  depth order – a station can never be visited after a deeper one.

* Suppose we are at depth `d` with `ox` oxygen left and the next
  station (or the cave) is at depth `nd` (`nd>d`).  
  We must cover the distance `dist = nd-d`.  
  If `ox ≥ dist` we can reach `nd` without refilling.

* If `ox < dist` we must use some of the stations we have already
  passed (their oxygen cannot be taken in the future).  
  To keep the total time minimal we should always use the *cheapest*
  refills first.

  This is exactly the same strategy that is used in the classical
  “Expedition” problem – the only difference is that the cost is
  **time** and the refill amount is fixed (`C`).

* While we travel, the only thing that matters for the future is the
  *set* of refills that are still unused and the oxygen that we keep.
  Therefore a greedy strategy that, whenever oxygen is insufficient,
  takes the cheapest unused refill is optimal.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.  Greedy algorithm

All stations are sorted by depth (ascending).

```
currentDepth = 0
ox          = O
totalTime   = 0
min‑heap H  – stores the times of stations that we have already passed
```

For every station `S` (in order) and finally for the cave (treated as
a virtual station with time `0`):

```
dist = S.depth - currentDepth

/* can we reach the next point without refilling ? */
while (ox < dist && H is not empty)
        take the cheapest refill from H
        ox       += C
        totalTime += time_of_this_refill

/* still not enough oxygen – we cannot reach S */
if (ox < dist)
        maxDepth = currentDepth + ox   // we run out after ox metres
        print "-1 maxDepth" and exit

/* we reach S */
ox          -= dist
currentDepth = S.depth

/* the station itself becomes available for future use */
push S.time into H
```

If the loop finishes successfully, we have reached the cave and
`totalTime` is the minimal time needed.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm prints

* the minimal possible total refilling time when the cave is reachable,
* otherwise the maximal depth that Percy can reach.

---

##### Lemma 1  
When Percy reaches a station or the cave, the set of stations that are
already passed and still unused is exactly the set stored in the heap
at that moment.

**Proof.**

The algorithm pushes a station’s time into the heap **only** when the
station is reached (after the subtraction of the travelled distance).
No station is ever removed from the heap except by the greedy
“take the cheapest refill” step, and this removal corresponds to
actually using that station.  
Hence the heap always contains exactly the stations that have been
passed and not yet used. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
If at some point Percy is at depth `d` with `ox` oxygen left and the
next point is at depth `nd`, and the algorithm refills exactly the
cheapest possible set of unused stations that makes `ox ≥ nd-d`,
then every other feasible strategy that reaches `nd` needs a total
refilling time **not smaller** than the algorithm’s time up to that
moment.

**Proof.**

Assume a different strategy `S` also reaches `nd`.  
Let the multiset of stations used by `S` be `R`.
Because `S` reaches `nd`, after adding the oxygen from all stations in
`R` we have at least `nd-d` oxygen, i.e.

```
ox + |R| * C  ≥  nd-d
```

Let `k = |R|`.  
The algorithm chooses the `k` cheapest unused stations, therefore the
sum of their times is ≤ the sum of times of any other `k` unused
stations, in particular ≤ the sum of times of the stations in `R`.  
Thus the algorithm’s total time up to depth `nd` is not larger than
`S`’s total time. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
If the algorithm stops at depth `d` because it cannot reach the next
station, then `d` is the maximum reachable depth.

**Proof.**

When the algorithm stops we have

```
ox + m*C < dist           (1)
```

where `m` is the number of stations that have been used so far and
`dist` the distance to the next point (`nd-d`).  
`ox + m*C` is exactly the amount of oxygen that is available after all
possible refills (Lemma&nbsp;1).  
Condition (1) means that even after using *all* unused stations we do
not have enough oxygen to reach `nd`.  
Therefore any strategy must stop before reaching `nd`.  
The furthest depth it can reach is `d + ox + m*C`, which is exactly
the depth printed by the algorithm. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
If the algorithm reaches the cave, the total time it reports is
minimal among all strategies that reach the cave.

**Proof.**

We use induction over the sequence of points (stations + cave).

*Base:*  
Before the first station the algorithm has zero refills – this is
trivially optimal.

*Induction step:*  
Assume that after reaching point `i-1` the algorithm has the minimal
possible total time.  
Let point `i` be the next station (or the cave).  
Both the algorithm and any optimal strategy must be able to reach
point `i`.  
When the algorithm reaches point `i`, it uses exactly the cheapest
possible set of unused stations (Lemma&nbsp;2).  
Therefore its additional time compared with the optimal strategy is
minimal, and by the induction hypothesis the whole total time remains
minimal.

Thus after the last point (the cave) the algorithm’s total time is
minimal. ∎



##### Theorem  
The algorithm outputs

1. the minimal total refilling time if the cave is reachable, and  
2. otherwise `-1` and the maximum depth Percy can reach.

**Proof.**

*If the algorithm reaches the cave*, by Lemma&nbsp;4 the reported
time is minimal.

*If the algorithm stops early*, by Lemma&nbsp;3 the printed depth is
the greatest one that can be reached.  
The algorithm prints `-1` together with that depth, satisfying the
required output. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Complexity Analysis

Let `N` be the number of stations.

*Sorting* the stations: `O(N log N)`  
*Processing* each station and the final cave: each station is pushed
once into the heap and each refill is popped at most once → `O(N log N)`.

```
Time   :  O(N log N)
Memory :  O(N)          (array of stations + heap)
```

Both limits satisfy the constraints (time limit 1 s, memory 128 MB).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (C)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef long long ll;

typedef struct {
    int depth;
    int time;
} Station;

int cmpStation(const void *a, const void *b) {
    return ((Station *)a)->depth - ((Station *)b)->depth;
}

/* --------- simple min‑heap for ints (times) --------- */
typedef struct {
    int *data;
    int sz;
    int cap;
} MinHeap;

void heapInit(MinHeap *h, int cap) {
    h->data = (int *)malloc(sizeof(int) * cap);
    h->sz   = 0;
    h->cap  = cap;
}

void heapPush(MinHeap *h, int val) {
    if (h->sz == h->cap) {           // should never happen
        h->cap <<= 1;
        h->data = (int *)realloc(h->data, sizeof(int) * h->cap);
    }
    int i = h->sz++;
    h->data[i] = val;
    while (i > 0) {
        int p = (i - 1) >> 1;
        if (h->data[p] <= h->data[i]) break;
        int t = h->data[p]; h->data[p] = h->data[i]; h->data[i] = t;
        i = p;
    }
}

int heapPop(MinHeap *h) {
    int res = h->data[0];
    int last = h->data[--h->sz];
    int i = 0, child;
    while ((child = (i << 1) + 1) < h->sz) {
        if (child + 1 < h->sz && h->data[child + 1] < h->data[child])
            child++;
        if (last <= h->data[child]) break;
        h->data[i] = h->data[child];
        i = child;
    }
    h->data[i] = last;
    return res;
}

int heapEmpty(MinHeap *h) { return h->sz == 0; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------- */

int main(void) {
    int N;
    ll O, F, C;
    if (scanf("%d %lld %lld %lld", &N, &O, &F, &C) != 4) return 0;

    Station *st = (Station *)malloc(sizeof(Station) * N);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) scanf("%d", &st[i].depth);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) scanf("%d", &st[i].time);

    qsort(st, N, sizeof(Station), cmpStation);

    MinHeap heap;
    heapInit(&heap, N + 5);

    ll curDepth = 0;
    ll ox       = O;
    ll totalTime = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        ll dist = (ll)st[i].depth - curDepth;

        while (ox < dist && !heapEmpty(&heap)) {
            int t = heapPop(&heap);
            ox += C;
            totalTime += t;
        }

        if (ox < dist) {          // cannot reach this station
            ll maxDepth = curDepth + ox;
            printf("-1 %lld\n", maxDepth);
            return 0;
        }

        ox -= dist;
        curDepth = st[i].depth;
        heapPush(&heap, st[i].time);
    }

    /* finally the cave */
    ll dist = F - curDepth;
    while (ox < dist && !heapEmpty(&heap)) {
        int t = heapPop(&heap);
        ox += C;
        totalTime += t;
    }

    if (ox < dist) {
        ll maxDepth = curDepth + ox;
        printf("-1 %lld\n", maxDepth);
        return 0;
    }

    ox -= dist;  // reached the cave
    printf("%lld\n", totalTime);
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the required C‑language standard.

