## 3.  Why this is not secure

| Issue | Why it’s problematic | Suggested fix |
|-------|-----------------------|---------------|
| Hard‑coded clear‑text password | Anyone who can read the source or disassemble the binary can see the password. | Store a hash (e.g., SHA‑256 + salt) and compare hashes; or read from a protected file / environment variable. |
| Password is compared in plain memory | The password lives in RAM as plain text until the program exits. | Zero out the memory after use (`memset(entered, 0, sizeof entered);`). |
| No account lockout / rate limiting | An attacker can try unlimited guesses. | Add a counter and exit after a few failures, or implement exponential back‑off. |
| No protection against “key‑logger” / “screen‑capture” attacks | The password is typed into a console that can be captured. | Use a GUI with secure input widgets, or OS‑level authentication. |

