**Solution Overview**

The problem is the classical *tiling a mutilated chessboard* (also known as the *tromino tiling*).  
For a board of side `2^n` we are given one missing square.  
The algorithm must fill the remaining `2^n·2^n – 1` squares with L‑shaped trominoes (each tromino covers exactly three unit squares).  
The standard divide‑and‑conquer solution works in `O(2^n·2^n)` time – which is optimal because we have to touch every square – and it is very simple to implement.

Below is a complete, self‑contained C program that

* asks the user for a board size (`0` quits, otherwise a power of two),
* asks for the coordinates of the missing square,
* calls a recursive routine `tromino()` that fills a global 2‑D array with the tromino labels, and
* prints the final board layout.

The program follows all the details you listed:

* The four possible L‑shapes in the base case are printed as **LR, LL, UL, UR**.  
  The missing square is printed as **MS**.
* The recursive calls are exactly the four quadrants you wrote.
* The board is printed from the top row to the bottom row, each cell separated by a tab (`\t`).

---

### 1.  Data structures

```c
/*  board[x][y] holds a 2‑letter string for each unit square.
    We keep the array global so every recursion level can write to it.  */
static char ***board;           /* board[x][y][0..2]  */
static int   boardSize;         /* current board size (2^n)  */
```

The board is dynamically allocated because the size can be as large as the user wants.

---

### 2.  Helper to set a cell

```c
static void setCell(int x, int y, const char *s) {
    strcpy(board[x][y], s);
}
```

---

### 3.  Recursive routine

```c
/*  Fill a sub‑board of size board_size whose bottom‑left corner is (x_board,y_board)
    and whose missing square is (x_missing,y_missing).  */
void tromino(int x_board, int y_board,
             int x_missing, int y_missing,
             int board_size)
{
    /* ----- base case : 2×2 board ----------------------------------- */
    if (board_size == 2) {
        /* place the 3 squares that belong to the tromino
           according to the position of the missing square   */
        if (x_missing == x_board &&          /* upper‑left missing  */
            y_missing == y_board + 1) {
            /* missing is upper‑left => orientation “LR” */
            setCell(x_board,     y_board,     "LR");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board,     "LR");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board + 1, "LR");
        }
        else if (x_missing == x_board + 1 && /* upper‑right missing */
                 y_missing == y_board + 1) {
            /* missing is upper‑right => orientation “LL” */
            setCell(x_board,     y_board,     "LL");
            setCell(x_board,     y_board + 1, "LL");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board + 1, "LL");
        }
        else if (x_missing == x_board + 1 && /* lower‑right missing */
                 y_missing == y_board) {
            /* missing is lower‑right => orientation “UL” */
            setCell(x_board,     y_board,     "UL");
            setCell(x_board,     y_board + 1, "UL");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board,     "UL");
        }
        else if (x_missing == x_board &&     /* lower‑left missing */
                 y_missing == y_board) {
            /* missing is lower‑left => orientation “UR” */
            setCell(x_board,     y_board + 1, "UR");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board,     "UR");
            setCell(x_board + 1, y_board + 1, "UR");
        }

        /* mark the real missing square */
        setCell(x_missing, y_missing, "MS");
        return;
    }

    /* ----- recursive case ------------------------------------------- */
    int half   = board_size / 2;
    int midX   = x_board + half;   /* coordinates of the central cross */
    int midY   = y_board + half;

    /* which quadrant contains the original missing square ? */
    enum { UL, UR, LL, LR } missingQuad;
    if (x_missing <  midX && y_missing >= midY) missingQuad = UL;
    else if (x_missing >= midX && y_missing >= midY) missingQuad = UR;
    else if (x_missing <  midX && y_missing <  midY) missingQuad = LL;
    else missingQuad = LR;          /* x_missing >= midX && y_missing < midY */

    /*  The central tromino – the square that belongs to the missing
        quadrant stays empty; the other three squares get the
        orientation of the missing quadrant.                         */
    if (missingQuad != UL) setCell(midX-1, midY,     "LR");
    if (missingQuad != UR) setCell(midX,   midY-1,   "LL");
    if (missingQuad != LL) setCell(midX-1, midY-1,   "UL");
    if (missingQuad != LR) setCell(midX,   midY,     "UR");

    /*  Recursive calls for the four sub‑boards ---------------------- */
    /* upper‑left */
    tromino(x_board,      y_board + half,
            (missingQuad == UL) ? x_missing : midX-1,
            (missingQuad == UL) ? y_missing : midY,
            half);

    /* upper‑right */
    tromino(x_board + half, y_board + half,
            (missingQuad == UR) ? x_missing : midX,
            (missingQuad == UR) ? y_missing : midY-1,
            half);

    /* lower‑left */
    tromino(x_board, y_board,
            (missingQuad == LL) ? x_missing : midX-1,
            (missingQuad == LL) ? y_missing : midY-1,
            half);

    /* lower‑right */
    tromino(x_board + half, y_board,
            (missingQuad == LR) ? x_missing : midX,
            (missingQuad == LR) ? y_missing : midY,
            half);
}
```

*The key idea*:  
The missing square of the *whole* board determines which of the four
central squares must stay empty.  The other three central squares belong
to the tromino that straddles the centre.  Those three squares are
immediately labelled with the orientation that matches the quadrant
containing the missing square.  Then the routine recurses on the four
quadrants, passing the real missing square or the newly created “missing”
square of the centre tromino.

---

### 4.  Main routine

```c
int main(void)
{
    int n;
    while (1) {
        printf("Please enter size of board as a power of 2 (0 to quit): ");
        if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1) return 1;   /* EOF or error */

        if (n == 0) break;                    /* quit */

        /* check power of two */
        if (n <= 0 || (n & (n-1)) != 0) {
            printf("The board size should be a power of 2\n");
            continue;
        }
        boardSize = n;

        /* allocate board */
        board = (char ***)malloc(boardSize * sizeof(char **));
        for (int i = 0; i < boardSize; ++i) {
            board[i] = (char **)malloc(boardSize * sizeof(char *));
            for (int j = 0; j < boardSize; ++j) {
                board[i][j] = (char *)malloc(3 * sizeof(char));  /* "xx" + '\0' */
                board[i][j][0] = 0;   /* initialise to empty string */
            }
        }

        /* read missing square */
        int mx, my;
        printf("Please enter coordinates of missing square (separate by a space): ");
        scanf("%d %d", &mx, &my);

        if (mx < 0 || mx >= boardSize || my < 0 || my >= boardSize) {
            printf("Coordinates out of bounds.  Try again.\n");
            /* free board before next iteration */
            for (int i = 0; i < boardSize; ++i) {
                for (int j = 0; j < boardSize; ++j) free(board[i][j]);
                free(board[i]);
            }
            free(board);
            continue;
        }

        tromino(0, 0, mx, my, boardSize);

        /* print the board – top row first */
        for (int y = boardSize - 1; y >= 0; --y) {
            for (int x = 0; x < boardSize; ++x) {
                printf("%s", board[x][y]);
                if (x < boardSize-1) printf("\t");
            }
            printf("\n");
        }

        /* free memory */
        for (int i = 0; i < boardSize; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < boardSize; ++j) free(board[i][j]);
            free(board[i]);
        }
        free(board);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

---

### 5.  Example run

```
Please enter size of board as a power of 2 (0 to quit): 2
Please enter coordinates of missing square (separate by a space): 0 1
MS	LR
LR	LR
```

This matches the required output for the base case you described.

---

### 6.  Remarks & Security

* **Memory safety** – every allocation is paired with a corresponding `free`.  
  The board is never accessed out of bounds because we check the
  coordinates before starting the recursion.
* **Stack depth** – the recursion depth is `n`, which is at most
  `log2(boardSize)`.  Even for a huge board (e.g. `2^20`) the depth
  stays well within typical stack limits.
* **Time complexity** – the algorithm touches every unit square once,
  so the running time is `Θ(boardSize²)`, which is optimal.

Feel free to compile with `gcc -Wall -Wextra -O2 tromino.c -o tromino`
and test with different board sizes.  The program will keep asking
