## 2.  Dead‑lock example in C

Dead‑lock happens when two or more processes/threads wait forever for each
other’s resources.  
Below is a small program that deliberately creates a dead‑lock between two
threads by acquiring the two semaphores in **opposite orders**.

```c
/* deadlock_demo.c */
#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <unistd.h>

static const char *SEM1_NAME = "/sem1";
static const char *SEM2_NAME = "/sem2";

static sem_t *sem1;
static sem_t *sem2;

void *thread_a(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    printf("[Thread A] Waiting on sem1 …\n");
    sem_wait(sem1);
    printf("[Thread A] Acquired sem1.\n");

    /* Simulate work and give the other thread a chance */
    sleep(1);

    printf("[Thread A] Waiting on sem2 …\n");
    sem_wait(sem2);
    printf("[Thread A] Acquired sem2.\n");

    /* Release in reverse order */
    sem_post(sem2);
    sem_post(sem1);
    printf("[Thread A] Released both semaphores.\n");
    return NULL;
}

void *thread_b(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    printf("[Thread B] Waiting on sem2 …\n");
    sem_wait(sem2);
    printf("[Thread B] Acquired sem2.\n");

    sleep(1);

    printf("[Thread B] Waiting on sem1 …\n");
    sem_wait(sem1);
    printf("[Thread B] Acquired sem1.\n");

    sem_post(sem1);
    sem_post(sem2);
    printf("[Thread B] Released both semaphores.\n");
    return NULL;
}

int main(void)
{
    sem1 = sem_open(SEM1_NAME, O_CREAT, 0666, 1);
    if (sem1 == SEM_FAILED) { perror("sem_open /sem1"); exit(1); }

    sem2 = sem_open(SEM2_NAME, O_CREAT, 0666, 1);
    if (sem2 == SEM_FAILED) { perror("sem_open /sem2"); exit(1); }

    pthread_t ta, tb;
    pthread_create(&ta, NULL, thread_a, NULL);
    pthread_create(&tb, NULL, thread_b, NULL);

    pthread_join(ta, NULL);
    pthread_join(tb, NULL);

    /* Clean up – leave the named semaphores in place for the controller
       program; you can optionally unlink them here if you run this demo
       alone. */
    sem_close(sem1);
    sem_close(sem2);

    /* Optional: sem_unlink(SEM1_NAME); sem_unlink(SEM2_NAME); */

    return 0;
}
```

### Compile

```bash
gcc -Wall -Wextra -pthread deadlock_demo.c -o deadlock_demo
```

### Run

```bash
./deadlock_demo
```

**What happens**

* Thread A acquires `sem1`, sleeps, then tries to acquire `sem2`.  
* Thread B acquires `sem2`, sleeps, then tries to acquire `sem1`.  
* After the sleeps both threads are waiting forever – a classic dead‑lock.
  The program will hang; terminate it with `Ctrl‑C` if you want to stop it.

You can also run `deadlock_demo` **after** the controller instances, to see
how the dead‑lock persists until you manually terminate the program.

---

## 3.  Cleanup notes

* The named semaphores (`/sem1`, `/sem2`) exist in the system until **one**
  process calls `sem_unlink`.  
  If you want to keep them between runs, **do not unlink** them until all
  programs using them exit.  
* The controller program does *not* unlink the semaphores – it only closes
  them.  
* If you want a fresh start, manually unlink them once:

```bash
sem_unlink /sem1
sem_unlink /sem2
```

or from within a program:

```c
