### Why this is “security‑safe”

| Concern | How the code addresses it |
|---------|--------------------------|
| **Buffer over‑run** | All copies use `memcpy`/`strcpy` with an explicit size (`max_len`). The size is derived from `sizeof(S1)`/`sizeof(S2)` – which is the compile‑time maximum capacity. No string can exceed that capacity. |
| **Use‑after‑free** | `malloc`/`free` are balanced, and the temporary buffer is only used while the function is executing. |
| **Unbounded string functions** | We avoid `strcpy`, `sprintf`, `gets`, etc. – the only functions that copy are size‑aware (`memcpy`). |
| **Stack overflow** | The heap‑based temporary (`malloc`) guarantees that we never overflow the stack for arbitrarily large strings. |
| **Aliasing / self‑swap** | `swap_using_temp` uses a temporary buffer, so swapping the same array with itself works fine (nothing will be overwritten). |
| **Pointer aliasing** | The pointer‑swap routine (`swap_pointers`) is safe *only* when you own the memory and do not keep any other pointer to the original memory. If you do, you’ll just have two aliases pointing to the same memory – not a “swap”. |

> **Note**  
> In your original snippet `char S1[] = "water";` and `char S2[] = "fire";` the arrays have lengths 6 and 5 respectively.  
> Swapping them with the temporary buffer method works because the temporary has size `max_len` = `sizeof(S1)` = 6.  
> If you plan to swap strings of arbitrary length, you can either:
> 1. Allocate a temporary buffer large enough for the **longest** string at runtime (`malloc` the maximum of the two `strlen()` + 1), or
> 2. Use `std::string` in C++ (if you switch languages), which hides all of these details and is automatically memory‑safe.

### Quick “pointer only” trick

If you really only need to exchange *where* the two variables point and you’re working with dynamically allocated strings, you can do:

```c
char *s1 = strdup("water");
char *s2 = strdup("fire");
char *tmp = s1; s1 = s2; s2 = tmp;   /* no copies, just pointer swaps */

printf("%s %s\n", s1, s2);   /* prints: fire water */

free(s1); free(s2);
```

This is the most efficient way (no data movement), but remember that `S1` and `S2` are no longer the original arrays – they’re now just pointers to the dynamically allocated memory. In many security contexts, copying the data (as in the first two functions) is safer because you retain the original memory layout and avoid dangling pointers.

