| Step | What to do | How |
|------|------------|-----|
| Generate DH params | `openssl dhparam -out dh2048.pem 2048` | |
| Prepare client cert/key | `openssl req -new -x509 -nodes -keyout client-key.pem -out client-cert.pem -days 365` | |
| Client code | Use the C snippet or Python example, set `-cipher DHE-RSA-AES256-SHA256`. | |
| Server code | Use the C snippet or Python example, set `-cipher DHE-NULL-3DES-CBC-SHA` and `-verify 0`. | |
| Compile / run | `gcc client.c -lssl -lcrypto` etc. | |
| Verify cipher | Print `SSL_get_cipher()` or `ssock.cipher()` output. | |

That’s it! You now have a minimal, reproducible setup that matches the professor’s constraints (as closely as OpenSSL allows).  
