Below is a **complete, self‑contained Java 11+ example** that shows how a security researcher (or any developer) can parse a **Jira XML export** file in a safe, memory‑efficient way.  
The code deliberately addresses the most common security pitfalls that appear when working with XML:

| Issue | Mitigation |
|-------|------------|
| **XML External Entity (XXE)** | Disable external entities and DOCTYPE parsing. |
| **Denial‑of‑Service via huge files** | Use a streaming parser (StAX) instead of DOM. |
| **Input validation / injection** | Treat all field values as plain text; never evaluate them as code. |
| **Logging sensitive data** | Never log raw issue bodies; redact or mask as needed. |
| **Resource exhaustion** | Use bounded streams, proper try‑with‑resources. |

