## 9️⃣ Security configuration

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SecurityConfig {

    private final JwtAuthFilter jwtAuthFilter;
    private final UserDetailsService userDetailsService;
    private final PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;

    @Bean
    public AuthenticationManager authManager(HttpSecurity http,
                                             UserDetailsService uds,
                                             PasswordEncoder pwdEncoder)
            throws Exception {
        return http.getSharedObject(AuthenticationManagerBuilder.class)
                   .userDetailsService(uds)
                   .passwordEncoder(pwdEncoder)
                   .and()
                   .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())                     // API only, JWT protects
            .sessionManagement(sess -> sess.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .authenticationProvider(new DaoAuthenticationProvider() {{
                setUserDetailsService(userDetailsService);
                setPasswordEncoder(passwordEncoder);
            }})
            .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);

        return http.build();
    }
}
```

> **Key points**  
> * Stateless session – no server‑side session stores.  
> * `csrf` disabled because we’re not using cookies for credentials (but keep in mind
>   you *could* enable CSRF protection if you decide to use cookies).  
> * `JwtAuthFilter` runs before `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter` so that
>   all authenticated endpoints get the token verified first.

