## 5. Building the SOAP/REST Envelope

### 5.1 SOAP (XML) Example

```java
String soapTemplate = """
    <soapenv:Envelope xmlns:soapenv="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"
                      xmlns:fisk="http://fiskalizacija.gov.hr/schemas">
        <soapenv:Header/>
        <soapenv:Body>
            {PAYLOAD}
        </soapenv:Body>
    </soapenv:Envelope>""";

Document signedDoc = ...; // from SignatureUtil.signXml
String signedXml = xmlToString(signedDoc);

String soapPayload = soapTemplate.replace("{PAYLOAD}", signedXml);
```

### 5.2 REST (JSON) Example

```json
{
  "invoice": {
    "header": { ... },
    "items": [ ... ]
  },
  "signature": "<base64‑encoded JWS>"
}
```

> **Remember:** The HDU requires a specific namespace (`http://fiskalizacija.gov.hr/schemas`) and element ordering; use the official WSDL/XSD for validation before sending.

---

## 6. Sending the Request – HTTPS with java.net.http

```java
package com.mycorp.fiskal;

import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.*;
import java.time.Duration;

public class HttpClientUtil {
    private final HttpClient client;

    public HttpClientUtil() {
        this.client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2)
                .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
                .build();
    }

    public HttpResponse<String> post(String url, String body, String contentType) throws Exception {
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create(url))
                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))
                .header("Content-Type", contentType)
                .header("Accept", "application/xml")   // or application/json
                .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(body))
                .build();

        return client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
    }
}
```

### TLS / Trust Store

If the HDU uses a custom CA, add its certificate to your **Java trust store**:

```bash
keytool -importcert -file hdu-ca.crt -keystore $JAVA_HOME/jre/lib/security/cacerts -storepass changeit
```

> **Tip:** For a production deployment, keep the trust store in a separate, read‑only volume and version‑control it via CI/CD.

