|---------|----------------|-----------------|-----|
| **Smart‑quotes (Unicode “ ”) instead of normal “"`** | The Java compiler treats them as ordinary characters, so the controller class does **not** compile. When the application starts the MVC subsystem can’t map `/users` etc., so the request falls back to the default error page. | If the source is compiled with the wrong characters you could be running **non‑existent** code; attackers can exploit a “broken” app to cause denial of service. | Replace every `“` and `”` with the ASCII `"` (double quote). |
| **Missing `@Entity` / `@Id` annotations on `User`** | Spring Data JPA cannot create the table or load entities. The `userRepository.findAll()` call throws a `PersistenceException`, which propagates as an unhandled `RuntimeException` → 500. | A failed query can leak stack traces (if you enable debug) and give clues about DB schema. | Add `@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Id` and `@GeneratedValue` to `User`. |
| **No validation or binding errors handling** | `UserService.saveUser` throws an `IllegalArgumentException` if the username is empty. The controller does **not** catch it, so the exception bubbles to the DispatcherServlet and becomes a 500. | The exception message may expose internal IDs or business logic. | Use `@Valid` + `BindingResult` in the controller; create a `@ControllerAdvice` that translates `IllegalArgumentException` into a user‑friendly error page or redirects back with a flash attribute. |
