Below is a **complete, runnable Java 17+ example** that shows how a “security‑researcher‑style” file indexer can:

1. Discover every root drive (C:\, D:\, /, …) on the local machine.  
2. Recursively walk each drive and build an in‑memory index that contains the file path, size, last‑modified timestamp and a simple hash.  
3. Persist that index to a JSON file (so you can restart the program without rescanning).  
4. Watch every directory for create, delete, modify events and keep the index up‑to‑date.  
5. Offer a simple text‑based search that queries the in‑memory index instead of walking the file system again.

> **Why this is useful** –  
> *From a security‑research perspective this is exactly the type of “baseline” inventory you’d build when you first
>  attach to a workstation, or when you’re writing a tool that needs to know “what is on this disk?” quickly.
>  The code is intentionally kept small (no external dependencies other than Jackson for JSON),
>  but the architecture is extensible enough that you could plug in a database, a full‑text search engine,
>  or a more sophisticated change‑tracking system later.*

---

## 1. Project Layout

```
src/
 └─ main/
     └─ java/
         └─ com.example.fileindexer/
              ├─ FileIndex.java
              ├─ FileInfo.java
              ├─ Indexer.java
              ├─ FileWatcher.java
              ├─ Searcher.java
              └─ Main.java
```

