## 3. The **TaskManager** (Desktop – Master)

### 3.1  Networking Layer

```java
// 3️⃣  TaskManager.java
package com.example.desktop;

import com.example.distrib.Task;
import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.security.KeyStore;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class TaskManager {
    private final ExecutorService dispatchPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
    private final ForkJoinPool computePool = ForkJoinPool.commonPool(); // or a custom one
    private final String workerHost;
    private final int workerPort;
    private final SSLContext sslCtx;

    public TaskManager(String workerHost, int workerPort) throws Exception {
        this.workerHost = workerHost;
        this.workerPort = workerPort;
        this.sslCtx = createSSLContext(); // load keystore, truststore
    }

    /* ---------- 1️⃣  Dispatch a task  ---------- */
    public Future<Object> dispatchTask(Task<?> task) {
        return dispatchPool.submit(() -> {
            try (SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) sslCtx.getSocketFactory()
                                                       .createSocket(workerHost, workerPort)) {
                socket.setSoTimeout(30_000); // 30s timeout

                try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());
                     ObjectInputStream in  = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {

                    // 3️⃣  Send the task
                    out.writeObject(task);
                    out.flush();

                    // 4️⃣  Await the result
                    Object result = in.readObject();
                    return result;
                }
            }
        });
    }

    /* ---------- 2️⃣  Build SSLContext  ---------- */
    private SSLContext createSSLContext() throws Exception {
        // Simple demo: load a single keystore containing a self‑signed cert.
        KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance("JKS");
        ks.load(new FileInputStream("keystore.jks"), "changeit".toCharArray());

        KeyManagerFactory kmf = KeyManagerFactory.getInstance("SunX509");
        kmf.init(ks, "changeit".toCharArray());

        TrustManagerFactory tmf = TrustManagerFactory.getInstance("SunX509");
        tmf.init(ks);

        SSLContext ctx = SSLContext.getInstance("TLSv1.3");
        ctx.init(kmf.getKeyManagers(), tmf.getTrustManagers(), null);
        return ctx;
    }

    /* ---------- 3️⃣  Shutdown helpers ---------- */
    public void shutdown() {
        dispatchPool.shutdown();
        computePool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### 3.2  Sample Main (Desktop)

```java
// 4️⃣  Main.java
package com.example.desktop;

import com.example.distrib.PrimeTask;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1️⃣  Connect to worker (Android) at 192.168.1.50:8443
        TaskManager manager = new TaskManager("192.168.1.50", 8443);

        // 2️⃣  Submit a batch of tasks
        int[] numbers = {999983, 123456, 987654, 42, 13};
        for (int n : numbers) {
            PrimeTask task = new PrimeTask(n);
            Future<Object> future = manager.dispatchTask(task);
            System.out.printf("Submitted task for %d, waiting...%n", n);
            // Non‑blocking; the thread pool will collect the result later
            manager.dispatchPool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    Boolean isPrime = (Boolean) future.get(); // blocking here
                    System.out.printf("%d is %s%n", n, isPrime ? "prime" : "not prime");
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            });
        }

        // 3️⃣  Gracefully shut down after all tasks finish
        manager.dispatchPool.awaitTermination(2, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
        manager.shutdown();
    }
}
```

> **What’s happening?**  
> * The desktop sends each `PrimeTask` over an SSL socket to the Android worker.  
> * The task is serialized via `ObjectOutputStream`.  
> * The worker runs it in a thread pool and streams back the Boolean result.  
> * The master uses a `Future` to block only when it wants the answer.

