Below is a *complete, production‑ready* recipe for turning a raw TCP/UDP Netty pipeline into a secure TLS/SSL channel.  
It covers both **server** and **client** sides, explains the key classes, shows how to choose the right provider, how to enforce the latest security best‑practices (TLS 1.2+ only, strong ciphers, mutual‑auth, ALPN, etc.), and how to hook into the handshake lifecycle.

> **TL;DR**  
> 1. Build an `SslContext` (server or client).  
> 2. Add `SslHandler` as the **first** handler in every pipeline.  
> 3. Configure cipher suites, protocols, and trust stores.  
> 4. Use the handshake future to react when TLS is ready.  
> 5. (Optional) enable ALPN for HTTP/2 or SNI for multi‑certificate hosts.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| Item | Why it matters | How to set it up |
|------|----------------|------------------|
| Netty dependencies | You need the core + handler modules. | ```maven
<dependency> <groupId>io.netty</groupId> <artifactId>netty-all</artifactId> <version>4.1.116.Final</version> </dependency>
``` |
| **Optional** OpenSSL native libs | Performance (hardware‑accelerated crypto) and ALPN. | ```maven
<dependency> <groupId>io.netty</groupId> <artifactId>netty-transport-native-epoll</artifactId> <version>4.1.116.Final</version> <classifier>linux-x86_64</classifier> </dependency>
``` (or kqueue for macOS) |
| Certificate & key | TLS requires a server cert and private key; client may need a trust store. | Self‑signed PEM/DER files or JKS. |
| JDK 11+ | Enables strong default TLS versions & cipher suites. | Install JDK 11/17/21. |

> **Tip:** For development use a self‑signed PEM pair (openssl `openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout key.pem -x509 -days 365 -out cert.pem`). For production, obtain a CA‑signed cert.

---

## 2. Server‑Side TLS

### 2.1. Building the SslContext

```java
import io.netty.handler.ssl.SslContext;
import io.netty.handler.ssl.SslContextBuilder;
import io.netty.handler.ssl.ClientAuth;
import io.netty.handler.ssl.SslProvider;
import java.io.File;

public class SslUtil {

    // Load server cert & key in PEM format
    public static SslContext serverContext() throws Exception {
        File certChain = new File("cert.pem");
        File privateKey = new File("key.pem");

        return SslContextBuilder.forServer(certChain, privateKey)
                // 1️⃣ Enforce TLS 1.2+ only
                .protocols("TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3")
                // 2️⃣ Use only strong ciphers (OpenSSL defaults are fine)
                .cipherSuiteFilter(SslContextBuilder.DEFAULT_CIPHER_SUITE_FILTER)
                // 3️⃣ (Optional) Mutual auth
                .trustManager(new File("ca-cert.pem"))   // client certs
                .clientAuth(ClientAuth.OPTIONAL)         // or .REQUIRE
                // 4️⃣ Prefer OpenSSL if available
                .sslProvider(SslProvider.isOpenSslAvailable() ?
                             SslProvider.OPENSSL : SslProvider.JDK)
                .build();
    }
}
```

*Why each setting?*

| Setting | Purpose | Recommendation |
|---------|---------|----------------|
| `protocols("TLSv1.2","TLSv1.3")` | Disables old TLS 1.0/1.1 & SSLv3. | **Required** for compliance. |
| `cipherSuiteFilter(...)` | Removes weak ciphers. | Keep defaults (OpenSSL/Java). |
| `trustManager(...)` | Enables client‑cert validation. | Only if mutual TLS is needed. |
| `clientAuth(...)` | Controls how strict client auth is. | `REQUIRE` for sensitive services. |
| `sslProvider(...)` | Selects JDK or native OpenSSL. | OpenSSL gives better perf & ALPN. |

### 2.2. Adding `SslHandler` to the pipeline

```java
import io.netty.channel.ChannelInitializer;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelPipeline;
import io.netty.channel.socket.SocketChannel;
import io.netty.handler.ssl.SslHandler;

public class SecureChannelInitializer extends ChannelInitializer<SocketChannel> {

    private final SslContext sslCtx;

    public SecureChannelInitializer(SslContext sslCtx) {
        this.sslCtx = sslCtx;
    }

    @Override
    protected void initChannel(SocketChannel ch) throws Exception {
        ChannelPipeline p = ch.pipeline();

        // 1️⃣ SSL must be the **first** handler
        SslHandler sslHandler = sslCtx.newHandler(ch.alloc());
        p.addLast(sslHandler);

        // 2️⃣ Optional: add a listener to know when TLS is done
        sslHandler.handshakeFuture().addListener(future -> {
            if (future.isSuccess()) {
                System.out.println("TLS handshake succeeded");
            } else {
                System.err.println("TLS handshake failed: " + future.cause());
                ch.close();
            }
        });

        // 3️⃣ Add your application handlers *after* SSL
        p.addLast(new HttpServerCodec());
        p.addLast(new HttpObjectAggregator(65536));
        p.addLast(new MyBusinessLogicHandler());
    }
}
```

**Important:** *Never* add any encoder/decoder that processes application data **before** the `SslHandler`. The TLS layer must handle encryption/decryption first.

### 2.3. Server bootstrap

```java
import io.netty.bootstrap.ServerBootstrap;
import io.netty.channel.nio.NioEventLoopGroup;
import io.netty.channel.socket.nio.NioServerSocketChannel;

public class SecureServer {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        SslContext ctx = SslUtil.serverContext();

        EventLoopGroup bossGroup = new NioEventLoopGroup(1);
        EventLoopGroup workerGroup = new NioEventLoopGroup();
        try {
            ServerBootstrap b = new ServerBootstrap();
            b.group(bossGroup, workerGroup)
             .channel(NioServerSocketChannel.class)
             .childHandler(new SecureChannelInitializer(ctx));

            b.bind(8443).sync().channel().closeFuture().sync();
        } finally {
            bossGroup.shutdownGracefully();
            workerGroup.shutdownGracefully();
        }
    }
}
```

