## 3.  Wrap the filter into a `WebFilter`

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.filter;

import com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis.RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.server.reactive.ServerHttpRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerResponse;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import org.springframework.web.server.WebFilter;
import org.springframework.web.server.WebFilterChain;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Configuration
public class RateLimiterWebFilterConfig {

    /**
     * A tiny bridge that turns the functional filter into a WebFilter.
     */
    @Bean
    public WebFilter rateLimiterWebFilter(RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction functionalFilter) {
        return (ServerWebExchange exchange, WebFilterChain chain) -> {
            ServerRequest serverRequest = ServerRequest.create(exchange, exchange.getAttributeNames());

            // The next function just forwards to the rest of the filter chain
            // and finally returns the ServerResponse that the controller method produced.
            return functionalFilter.filter(serverRequest, req ->
                    chain.filter(exchange)
                            // `chain.filter()` returns Mono<Void>, so we need to convert it
                            // into a ServerResponse.  The response is already written by
                            // the controller, so we just return a 200 OK placeholder.
                            // The actual response body is sent by the controller.
                            .then(ServerResponse.ok().build())
            );
        };
    }
}
```

### Why this works

* `WebFilter` receives the low‑level `ServerWebExchange`.  
* We create a `ServerRequest` from it – that’s what the functional filter expects.  
* The *next* handler is implemented by invoking `chain.filter(exchange)`, which continues the normal Spring WebFlux request flow.  
* Because `chain.filter()` only gives us a `Mono<Void>`, we convert it into a `ServerResponse` that signals “everything is OK”. The real body is produced by the controller; the placeholder is just a type‑compatibility trick.

> **Alternative** – If you want the response body *exactly* as produced by the controller, you can skip the `then(ServerResponse.ok().build())` part and let `chain.filter(exchange)` return a `Mono<ServerResponse>` directly. In that case you need a small custom `WebFilter` that captures the response body. The approach above is simpler and already works for the typical “rate‑limit before controller” scenario.

---

## 4.  Put it all together

1. **Add the starter dependency** (if you haven’t already):

   ```xml
   <dependency>
     <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
     <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis-reactive</artifactId>
   </dependency>
   ```

2. **Place the three classes** (`RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction`, `ReactiveRedisConfig`, `RateLimiterWebFilterConfig`) in the appropriate packages (`com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis` etc.).

3. **Define the property** in `application.yml` (or `application.properties`):

   ```yaml
   MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE: 20
   ```

4. **Run the application** – the filter is now active for *every* request.

> **What if I only want it for a specific controller?**  
> Instead of a global `WebFilter`, create a `RouterFunction<ServerResponse>` that wraps only the routes you want:

   ```java
   @Bean
   public RouterFunction<ServerResponse> rateLimitedRoutes(RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction rlFilter,
                                                           MyRestController controller) {
       return RouterFunctions.route()
               .GET("/my-endpoint", rlFilter.andRoute(request -> controller.myEndpoint(request)))
               // add more routes …
               .build();
   }
   ```

   This keeps the filter local to those routes and leaves the rest of the application unaffected.

---

## 5.  Quick sanity‑check

```bash
# Make sure Redis is running
docker run --rm -p 6379:6379 redis

# Start the Spring Boot app
./mvnw spring-boot:run

# Send 25 requests in the same minute
for i in {1..25}; do
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:8080/my-endpoint
done
```

You should see the first 20 responses return **200**, the last 5 return **429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS**.

---

## 6.  Recap

| What you did | Why it matters |
|--------------|----------------|
| Fixed the filter class to be a proper Spring bean | Enables dependency injection & property resolution |
| Created a dedicated Redis template bean | Keeps configuration clean |
| Wrapped the functional filter into a WebFilter | Makes it compatible with annotation‑based controllers |
| (Optional) used a per‑route RouterFunction | Gives fine‑grained control if you don’t want a global rate limit |

That’s it – you now have a **reactive, transactional‑style rate limiter** that works out of the box with Spring WebFlux controllers, without needing Spring Cloud Gateway. Happy hacking!

