Below is a self‑contained **Java** snippet that:

1. Connects to a target HTTPS site.  
2. Forces the TLS handshake to be performed.  
3. Logs the cipher suite that was negotiated.  
4. Detects whether Diffie‑Hellman (DH / DHE) or Elliptic‑Curve Diffie‑Hellman (ECDHE) was used.  
5. Optionally checks the key‑exchange group size (e.g. 1024‑bit DH = weak).

> **Why this is useful**  
> Many modern servers still advertise weak DH groups (e.g. `TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256` with 1024‑bit DH). Detecting this early helps you harden the server or choose an alternative cipher.

---

## 1. Full Example (Java 17+)

```java
import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URI;
import java.security.cert.Certificate;
import java.security.cert.X509Certificate;
import java.util.*;

public class DiffieHellmanCheck {

    /** 
     * Main entry point: java DiffieHellmanCheck https://example.com
     */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        if (args.length != 1) {
            System.err.println("Usage: java DiffieHellmanCheck https://<host>");
            System.exit(1);
        }

        URI uri = new URI(args[0]);
        if (!"https".equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getScheme())) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Only HTTPS is supported");
        }

        String host = uri.getHost();
        int port = uri.getPort() < 0 ? 443 : uri.getPort();

        // 1. Create SSLContext that accepts *all* certificates
        //    (for research only – never do this in production!)
        SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
        sslContext.init(
            null,
            new TrustManager[] { new X509TrustAllManager() },
            new java.security.SecureRandom()
        );

        // 2. Create SSLSocket and trigger handshake
        try (SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) sslContext.getSocketFactory()
                .createSocket(host, port)) {

            // Optional: set the *preferred* cipher suites
            // socket.setEnabledCipherSuites(preferredCiphers);

            // Force the TLS handshake
            socket.startHandshake();

            // 3. Retrieve the negotiated cipher suite
            String cipherSuite = socket.getSession().getCipherSuite();
            System.out.printf("Negotiated cipher suite: %s%n", cipherSuite);

            // 4. Detect Diffie‑Hellman usage
            boolean isDH = cipherSuite.contains("DHE") || cipherSuite.contains("DH");
            boolean isECDHE = cipherSuite.contains("ECDHE");

            if (isDH || isECDHE) {
                System.out.println("✅ Diffie‑Hellman key‑exchange detected");
                // 5. (Optional) Inspect key size
                int keySize = getKeySizeFromSession(socket.getSession());
                System.out.printf("   Key size: %d bits%n", keySize);
                if (keySize < 2048) {
                    System.out.println("⚠️  Weak DH key size – consider upgrading to 2048+ bits");
                }
            } else {
                System.out.println("❌ No Diffie‑Hellman key‑exchange (RSA or ECDSA direct)");
            }

            // 6. Show server certificates (just for completeness)
            printCertificates(socket.getSession().getPeerCertificates());
        }
    }

    /** 
     * Simple TrustManager that accepts every certificate.
     * ⚠️ Do NOT use this in production!
     */
    private static class X509TrustAllManager implements X509TrustManager {
        public void checkClientTrusted(X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) { }
        public void checkServerTrusted(X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) { }
        public X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return new X509Certificate[0]; }
    }

    /** Extract the key size from the SSLSession if available. */
    private static int getKeySizeFromSession(SSLSession session) {
        // Try to get the local cipher suite and parse the key size
        // For TLS 1.3 the key size is implicit in the cipher suite
        try {
            String cipher = session.getCipherSuite();
            // E.g. TLS_DHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
            if (cipher.contains("_WITH_AES_")) {
                // Look for the group name part
                // For simplicity, use known mapping:
                Map<String, Integer> dhGroups = Map.of(
                    "DHE_RSA", 1024,
                    "DHE_DSS", 1024,
                    "ECDHE_RSA", 256,
                    "ECDHE_ECDSA", 256,
                    "ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256", 256
                );
                for (String key : dhGroups.keySet()) {
                    if (cipher.contains(key)) {
                        return dhGroups.get(key);
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // ignore
        }
        return -1; // unknown
    }

    /** Pretty‑print the server certificates. */
    private static void printCertificates(Certificate[] certs) {
        System.out.println("Server certificate chain:");
        int i = 1;
        for (Certificate cert : certs) {
            if (cert instanceof X509Certificate) {
                X509Certificate x509 = (X509Certificate) cert;
                System.out.printf("  #%d: %s%n", i, x509.getSubjectDN());
                System.out.printf("      Issuer: %s%n", x509.getIssuerDN());
                System.out.printf("      Valid: %s – %s%n",
                        x509.getNotBefore(), x509.getNotAfter());
                i++;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| `SSLContext` | Uses the default TLS protocol (TLS 1.3/TLS 1.2). The custom `TrustManager` accepts any cert so the script can run against self‑signed certs. |
| `SSLSocket` | Opens a socket to the target host/port. |
| `startHandshake()` | Forces a full TLS handshake. |
| `getCipherSuite()` | Returns the negotiated suite (e.g. `TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256`). |
| Key‑exchange detection | The suite name contains `DHE`/`DH` for traditional DH or `ECDHE`/`ECDSA` for elliptic‑curve DH. |
| Key‑size check | Rough mapping; for real production use, parse the DH parameters from the server certificate or use BouncyCastle/ASN.1 parsing to get the exact key size. |
| Certificate dump | Gives you the full chain for manual inspection. |

---

## 2. Running the script

1. Compile:

   ```bash
   javac DiffieHellmanCheck.java
   ```

2. Run:

   ```bash
   java DiffieHellmanCheck https://example.com
   ```

You’ll see output similar to:

```
Negotiated cipher suite: TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
✅ Diffie‑Hellman key‑exchange detected
   Key size: 256 bits
⚠️  Weak DH key size – consider upgrading to 2048+ bits
Server certificate chain:
  #1: CN=example.com, O=Example Ltd, C=US
      Issuer: CN=Example CA, O=Example CA, C=US
      Valid: Jan 1 00:00:00 2023 – Jan 1 00:00:00 2024
