Below is a **complete, minimal Java servlet** that implements a secure file‑upload endpoint.  
The example uses the Apache Commons FileUpload library (the most common and well‑maintained approach in the Java ecosystem).  
All the usual hardening steps are shown:

| Hardening measure | Where it lives in the code |
|--------------------|----------------------------|
| **`@MultipartConfig`** | Servlet annotation – limits request size, temporary storage location. |
| **Maximum file size / request size** | `@MultipartConfig` attributes & explicit checks in code. |
| **Content‑type / MIME type validation** | `FileItem.getContentType()` & a whitelist. |
| **File name sanitization** | `Path` operations to strip directory separators. |
| **Unique storage name** | UUID‑based name + original extension. |
| **Storage directory** | Absolute path on the server (not user‑controlled). |
| **File permissions** | Java 7+ `Files.setPosixFilePermissions()` (Unix) or `File#setWritable` (cross‑platform). |
| **Error handling** | Clear, generic messages to the client; detailed logs for the dev. |

> **Why the extra safety checks?**  
> Even a “simple” file‑upload endpoint can be abused for:  
> •  directory traversal / path injection  
> •  overwriting important server files  
> •  uploading executables or scripts that the server can run  
> •  DoS by huge files or too many uploads  
> The code below follows best practice patterns that are widely accepted in the Java community.

---

## 1. Maven dependencies

Add the following to your `pom.xml`.  
If you use Gradle, the same artifacts apply.

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Apache Commons FileUpload -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>commons-fileupload</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-fileupload</artifactId>
        <version>1.5</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Apache Commons IO (used for extension extraction) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>commons-io</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-io</artifactId>
        <version>2.13.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

---

## 2. The Servlet

```java
package com.example.upload;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermission;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.annotation.MultipartConfig;
import javax.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

import org.apache.commons.fileupload.*;
import org.apache.commons.fileupload.disk.*;
import org.apache.commons.fileupload.servlet.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.FilenameUtils;

@WebServlet(name = "SecureFileUploadServlet", urlPatterns = { "/upload" })
@MultipartConfig(
        // Max size of a single uploaded file (bytes)
        maxFileSize = 5 * 1024 * 1024,        // 5 MiB
        // Max size of the entire request (bytes)
        maxRequestSize = 10 * 1024 * 1024,    // 10 MiB
        // Temporary location for data that exceeds the above limits
        location = "/tmp/commons_fileupload_tmp"
)
public class SecureFileUploadServlet extends HttpServlet {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(SecureFileUploadServlet.class.getName());

    /** Directory where uploaded files will be stored. */
    private static final Path STORAGE_DIR = Paths.get("/var", "webapp", "uploads");

    /** Allowed MIME types (simple whitelist). */
    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES = Set.of(
            "image/png",
            "image/jpeg",
            "image/gif",
            "application/pdf",
            "text/plain"
    );

    @Override
    public void init() throws ServletException {
        try {
            if (!Files.exists(STORAGE_DIR)) {
                Files.createDirectories(STORAGE_DIR, PosixFilePermissions.asFileAttribute(
                        PosixFilePermissions.fromString("rwxr-x---")
                ));
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            LOG.severe("Could not create storage directory: " + e.getMessage());
            throw new ServletException("Could not create storage directory", e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Verify that the request contains multipart content
        if (!ServletFileUpload.isMultipartContent(request)) {
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "Form must have enctype=multipart/form-data.");
            return;
        }

        // Build the file upload handler
        DiskFileItemFactory factory = new DiskFileItemFactory();
        // Use the same temp directory as declared in @MultipartConfig
        factory.setRepository(new File("/tmp/commons_fileupload_tmp"));
        ServletFileUpload upload = new ServletFileUpload(factory);

        // The library will still enforce @MultipartConfig limits; we check again for safety
        upload.setFileSizeMax(5 * 1024 * 1024L);          // 5 MiB
        upload.setSizeMax(10 * 1024 * 1024L);             // 10 MiB

        try {
            for (FileItem item : upload.parseRequest(request)) {

                if (!item.isFormField()) {

                    // ---- 1. Basic validations ----
                    // 1a. MIME type check
                    String mimeType = item.getContentType();
                    if (!ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES.contains(mimeType)) {
                        LOG.warning("Rejected file with disallowed MIME type: " + mimeType);
                        response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNSUPPORTED_MEDIA_TYPE,
                                "File type not allowed.");
                        return;
                    }

                    // 1b. File size check (already enforced by upload.setFileSizeMax,
                    //      but we double‑check in case of older servlet containers)
                    if (item.getSize() > 5 * 1024 * 1024) {
                        LOG.warning("Rejected oversized file: " + item.getSize());
                        response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_REQUEST_ENTITY_TOO_LARGE,
                                "File exceeds maximum allowed size.");
                        return;
                    }

                    // ---- 2. File name sanitization ----
                    String originalName = Paths.get(item.getName()).getFileName().toString(); // removes path
                    String sanitizedBase = originalName.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9-_\\.]", "_");
                    // Preserve extension (if any)
                    String ext = FilenameUtils.getExtension(sanitizedBase);
                    String baseWithoutExt = FilenameUtils.getBaseName(sanitizedBase);

                    // ---- 3. Generate a unique file name ----
                    String uniqueId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
                    String storedFileName = baseWithoutExt + "_" + uniqueId + (ext.isEmpty() ? "" : "." + ext);

                    Path targetPath = STORAGE_DIR.resolve(storedFileName);

                    // ---- 4. Write to disk ----
                    try (InputStream in = item.getInputStream()) {
                        Files.copy(in, targetPath, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
                    }

                    // ---- 5. Set safe file permissions (Unix only) ----
                    try {
                        Set<PosixFilePermission> perms = PosixFilePermissions.fromString("rw-r-----");
                        Files.setPosixFilePermissions(targetPath, perms);
                    } catch (UnsupportedOperationException uoe) {
                        // Windows doesn't support PosixFilePermissions; ignore
                    }

                    // ---- 6. Respond success ----
                    response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_OK);
                    response.getWriter().write("File uploaded successfully as " + storedFileName);
                    return;

                } else {
                    // Ignore other form fields; you can process them if needed
                }
            }

            // If we get here, no file was found in the request
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "No file part in the request.");
        } catch (FileUploadException e) {
            LOG.severe("File upload error: " + e.getMessage());
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                    "Error processing the file upload.");
        }
    }
}
```

### How the servlet works

| Step | Purpose | Notes |
|------|---------|-------|
| **`@MultipartConfig`** | Declares the max sizes and temp dir used by the servlet container before any Java code runs. | The servlet container checks these limits before parsing the request. |
| **`init()`** | Ensures the permanent storage directory exists and has safe permissions. | We create `/var/webapp/uploads` with `rwxr-x---`. |
| **`doPost()`** | Handles the actual file‑upload logic. | 1) Checks multipart; 2) Parses; 3) Validates MIME type & size; 4) Sanitizes name; 5) Generates unique name; 6) Stores; 7) Sets permissions; 8) Replies. |
| **Whitelist** | Only a handful of MIME types are accepted. | Expand or replace with a regex or magic‑byte check for production. |
