## 3. Detailed design

### 3.1  `Sondage` – the business object

* Immutable candidate list (`List<String>`) – no one can add/remove after
  construction.
* Vote counts stored in a `ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, AtomicInteger>`
  – key = candidate index, value = current count.
* All public methods are **thread‑safe**; no explicit `synchronized`
  needed because we use concurrent primitives.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.*;

public class Sondage {

    private final List<String> candidats;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, AtomicInteger> votes;

    public Sondage(List<String> candidats) {
        // defensive copy + unmodifiable wrapper
        this.candidats = List.copyOf(candidats);
        this.votes = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < candidats.size(); i++) {
            votes.put(i, new AtomicInteger(0));
        }
    }

    /** Return the list of candidate names */
    public List<String> getCandidats() {
        return candidats;          // unmodifiable
    }

    /** Vote for candidate index n (0‑based). */
    public boolean vote(int n) {
        AtomicInteger counter = votes.get(n);
        if (counter == null) {
            return false;          // invalid index
        }
        counter.incrementAndGet();
        return true;
    }

    /** Return the name of the most voted candidate.
        Ties are broken by lowest index. */
    public String getResultat() {
        int maxIdx = -1;
        int maxVotes = -1;
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, AtomicInteger> e : votes.entrySet()) {
            int cnt = e.getValue().get();
            if (cnt > maxVotes) {
                maxVotes = cnt;
                maxIdx = e.getKey();
            }
        }
        return (maxIdx == -1) ? "Aucun vote" : candidats.get(maxIdx);
    }
}
```

### 3.2  `Server` – acceptor + thread pool

* Uses a **bounded** `ThreadPoolExecutor` to avoid unlimited thread
  creation.  
  Example: 10 core threads, max 20, queue 50.
* Each accepted socket is handed to a `ClientHandler` that runs in the
  pool.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Server {

    private static final int PORT = 5000;
    private static final int CORE_POOL_SIZE = 10;
    private static final int MAX_POOL_SIZE = 20;
    private static final int KEEP_ALIVE = 60;          // seconds
    private static final int QUEUE_CAPACITY = 50;

    private final Sondage sondage;
    private final ExecutorService pool;

    public Server(Sondage sondage) {
        this.sondage = sondage;
        this.pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                CORE_POOL_SIZE,
                MAX_POOL_SIZE,
                KEEP_ALIVE,
                TimeUnit.SECONDS,
                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(QUEUE_CAPACITY),
                new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy()   // rejects new tasks when full
        );
    }

    public void start() throws IOException {
        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(PORT)) {
            System.out.println("Server listening on port " + PORT);
            while (true) {
                Socket client = serverSocket.accept();
                pool.submit(new ClientHandler(client, sondage));
            }
        } finally {
            pool.shutdown();
        }
    }

    /* --------------------------- */
    private static class ClientHandler implements Runnable {
        private final Socket socket;
        private final Sondage sondage;

        ClientHandler(Socket socket, Sondage sondage) {
            this.socket = socket;
            this.sondage = sondage;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            try (BufferedReader in  = new BufferedReader(
                     new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
                 BufferedWriter out = new BufferedWriter(
                     new OutputStreamWriter(socket.getOutputStream()))) {

                String line;
                while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    String response = handle(line.trim());
                    out.write(response);
                    out.newLine();
                    out.flush();
                }
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.err.println("Client connection error: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                try { socket.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            }
        }

        /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
        private String handle(String command) {
            if (command.equalsIgnoreCase("candidats")) {
                List<String> cand = sondage.getCandidats();
                return String.join(",", cand);
            } else if (command.toLowerCase().startsWith("vote ")) {
                try {
                    int n = Integer.parseInt(command.split("\\s+")[1]) - 1; // 1‑based for client
                    boolean ok = sondage.vote(n);
                    return ok ? "OK" : "ERR";
                } catch (NumberFormatException | ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException ex) {
                    return "ERR";
                }
            } else if (command.equalsIgnoreCase("resultat")) {
                return sondage.getResultat();
            } else {
                return "UNKNOWN";
            }
        }
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        List<String> candidates = List.of(
                "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Diana");
        Server server = new Server(new Sondage(candidates));
        server.start();
    }
}
```

> **Why `ArrayBlockingQueue` + `AbortPolicy`?**  
> If the pool is saturated, a new client will receive a
> `RejectedExecutionException`. The server can then close the
> socket, signalling the client to try again later – a simple
> back‑pressure mechanism.

### 3.3  `Client` – console helper

* Connects to `localhost:5000` (configurable).
* Reads user input, sends command, prints server response.
* Very small, but you can easily replace the console with a GUI or
  web front‑end.

```java
