Below is a minimal, working example that keeps your logic mostly intact but fixes the missing pieces and cleans up a few quirks.

---

## 1. What the original code was missing

| Problem | Why it matters | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| `handleSuccess` is defined but never called | No data ever reaches the rendering logic | Call it from the AJAX success handler |
| `divs` is never appended to the page | You create elements but never insert them into the DOM | `forumContent.appendChild(divs)` |
| No closing brace for `loadcontent` | Syntax error – the code you posted actually wouldn’t run | Add the final `}` |
| `texts[j]` inside the inner loop can be out of bounds | `texts` is the *entire* file split on `\n`, while the inner loop iterates over lines of *one* section (`textdata[i]`). | Use the same array that you split for the current section |
| `u` element is not recommended for underlining (semantic issues) | It works, but using `<strong>`/`<em>` or CSS is better | Replace with `<strong>` or style with CSS |
| Hard‑coded `length‑2` logic for detecting a trailing “:” | Fragile, can misbehave with short lines or whitespace | Use `trim().endsWith(':')` |
| No error handling for the request | Silent failures | Add a `.catch()` |

---

## 2. A clean, working version

```html
<!-- Your page -->
<div id="forum-content"></div>

<script>
/**
 * Fetches a text file and displays its content inside #forum-content.
 *
 * The file is expected to be a plain‑text file where sections are
 * separated by a single tilde `~` and each section consists of
 * one or more lines.
 */
function loadcontent() {
  const forumContent = document.getElementById('forum-content');
  forumContent.innerHTML = '';   // clear any existing content

  // ------- 1. AJAX request -------
  fetch('/path/to/your/file.txt')   // <-- change to your real URL
    .then(r => {
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
      return r.text();
    })
    .then(handleSuccess)
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to load content:', err));
}

// ------- 2. Success handler -------
function handleSuccess(response) {
  console.log('Raw response:', response);

  // split into sections by "~", then each section into lines
  const sections = response.split('~').map(sec => sec.trim()).filter(Boolean);

  const forumContent = document.getElementById('forum-content');

  sections.forEach(section => {
    const lines = section.split('\n').map(l => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
    const div = document.createElement('div');

    lines.forEach(line => {
      // Detect a line that ends with ":" (after trimming)
      if (line.endsWith(':')) {
        const strong = document.createElement('strong');
        strong.textContent = `>>> ${line}`;
        div.appendChild(strong);
      } else {
        const p = document.createElement('p');
        p.textContent = line;
        div.appendChild(p);
      }
    });

    forumContent.appendChild(div);   // *** ADD TO PAGE ***
  });
}

// Optionally call it once the page loads
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', loadcontent);
</script>
```

### Why this works

1. **`fetch()`** loads the file.  
   *If you’re using a different AJAX method (XHR, Axios, etc.), just replace the fetch block with the equivalent success handler.*

2. **`handleSuccess`** receives the *plain text* of the file.

3. The text is split into sections (`~`) and lines (`\n`).

4. For each section you create a `<div>` and append **either** a `<strong>` element (for lines ending in `:`) **or** a `<p>` element.  
   The `<div>` is appended to `#forum-content`, so the browser actually renders it.

5. All text nodes are inserted using `textContent` (or `innerText`) instead of `innerHTML`—no XSS risk from the file.

---

## 3. Quick debugging checklist

| Step | What to check | How |
|------|---------------|-----|
| **Is the AJAX call firing?** | Open DevTools → Network tab | Verify the request appears and returns a 200. |
| **Is `handleSuccess` called?** | Add `console.log('handleSuccess called')` | Check the console. |
| **Is `#forum-content` present?** | Inspect the DOM | The element must exist before you call `getElementById`. |
| **Are elements appended?** | Inspect `#forum-content` after load | You should see `<div>...</div>` inside. |
| **Are there any runtime errors?** | Console | Even if there are no syntax errors, logic errors (e.g., `texts[j]` undefined) will appear. |

If after adding the fetch and the `appendChild` you still see nothing, open the browser console and look for any thrown exceptions or failed network requests. Those are usually the first clues.

---

## 4. Optional improvements

| Feature | Why it helps | Quick tip |
|---------|--------------|-----------|
| **Debounce rapid reloads** | Prevents multiple overlapping requests | Use a flag or `lodash.debounce` |
| **Cache the response** | Avoids refetching on every load | Store the result in a variable |
| **Use CSS for styling** | Keeps JS free of presentation | E.g. `.section-title {font-weight: bold;}` |
| **Escape user‑generated content** | Prevent XSS | Use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` |

---

### Bottom line

Your code logic was fine; you just never *inserted* the created elements into the page and never actually fetched the file.  
Add the AJAX call, append the `<div>` to `#forum-content`, close the missing braces, and you’ll see your text rendered. Happy hacking!

