## 🔐  Security‑first Node JS skill‑listing endpoint

> **Goal** – Expose a *GET* endpoint that returns the list of skills a user has registered in the **`ExpertSkills`** table (or, if you prefer, a deduplicated list of all skills in the platform).

> **Why security matters** – Even a read‑only endpoint can be a target for:

| Threat | Mitigation | Why it matters |
|--------|------------|----------------|
| **SQL Injection** | Use prepared statements / parameterized queries. | Attackers could tamper with the query. |
| **Information Leakage** | Only return the data the caller is allowed to see. | Prevent over‑exposure of sensitive data. |
| **Rate‑Limiting / DoS** | Throttle requests. | Keep the API usable for legitimate users. |
| **Open Redirect / CSRF** | Use `helmet`, `cors`, and proper headers. | Basic web‑app security hygiene. |
| **Authentication & Authorization** | JWT / session token. | Only logged‑in users can see their own skills. |
| **Error Handling** | Never expose stack traces. | Avoid leaking internal details. |

Below is a **minimal, production‑ready** implementation that demonstrates all of the above.  
Feel free to cherry‑pick or extend the snippets.

---

### 1️⃣  Project Skeleton (ES‑M6)

```
/project
├─ src/
│  ├─ db.js          # DB pool
│  ├─ auth.js        # JWT middleware
│  ├─ routes/
│  │  └─ skills.js   # /skills endpoint
│  └─ app.js         # Express app
├─ .env
├─ package.json
└─ README.md
```

> **Node 18+** is assumed – it ships with native ES modules (`import`) and `top-level await`.  
> If you’re on <18, use `ts-node` or transpile.

