Below is a **safe, DOM‑based** helper that

1. Accepts an arbitrary string that may contain `<b>`, `<i>`, `<u>` (and their semantic counterparts)  
2. Detects *which* of those tags are present (even if the tags are nested or repeated)  
3. Builds a **new element** that holds the plain text **and** the combined style  
4. Returns that element so you can insert it wherever you like.

```html
<!-- example usage -->
<div id="target"></div>
<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  The helper ---------------------------------------------------
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  /**
   * Turns a string that may contain <b>/<i>/<u> into a styled span
   * while stripping out the markup itself.
   *
   * @param {string} rawHtml   The raw string (e.g. "<b><i>hello</i></b>")
   * @returns {HTMLElement}   A <span> element containing the plain text
   *                          with the combined style.
   */
  function stripAndStyle(rawHtml) {
    // 1. Parse the string safely into a temporary container
    const temp = document.createElement('div');
    temp.innerHTML = rawHtml;          // DOM handles the tags

    // 2. Detect which styling tags are present
    const hasBold   = temp.querySelector('b, strong') !== null;
    const hasItalic = temp.querySelector('i, em')       !== null;
    const hasUnder  = temp.querySelector('u')           !== null;

    // 3. Build the style string
    const styleParts = [];
    if (hasBold)   styleParts.push('font-weight:bold');
    if (hasItalic) styleParts.push('font-style:italic');
    if (hasUnder)  styleParts.push('text-decoration:underline');
    const combinedStyle = styleParts.join('; ') + (styleParts.length ? ';' : '');

    // 4. Grab the plain text (all tags removed)
    const plainText = temp.textContent || '';

    // 5. Create the final element
    const span = document.createElement('span');
    if (combinedStyle) span.style.cssText = combinedStyle;
    span.textContent = plainText;

    // 6. Return
    return span;
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Example usage ------------------------------------------------
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const raw = '<b><i>My test text</i></b>';   // <-- input
  const styled = stripAndStyle(raw);          // <-- <span> with styles

  // Insert wherever you want
  document.getElementById('target').appendChild(styled);
</script>
```

### Why this approach is safe

| Concern | What this code does |
|---------|---------------------|
| **XSS / script injection** | The string is parsed by the browser’s `innerHTML` into a DOM fragment. The fragment is **not** appended to the document until we explicitly create a clean `<span>` and copy only the text. No user‑supplied script tags get executed. |
| **Incorrect parsing with regex** | The DOM correctly handles nested tags, self‑closing tags, whitespace, etc. A regex would break on nested `<b><i>…</i></b>` or on malformed markup. |
| **Future-proofing** | If you later want to support `<strong>`, `<em>`, or other formatting tags, just add the selector and style mapping in the same place. |

### Extending the helper

If you need more tags (e.g., `<small>`, `<big>`, `<del>`), just add another boolean flag and the appropriate CSS property.

```js
const hasSmall = temp.querySelector('small') !== null;
if (hasSmall) styleParts.push('font-size:.8em');   // example
```

### Handling *multiple* styles

Because we query the container for each tag **once**, the flags are *true* if *any* such tag appears anywhere in the fragment. That means `<b><i>foo</i></b>` and `<b>bar<u>baz</u></b>` both get the correct combined style, no matter the nesting order.

Feel free to tweak the style mapping (e.g., use `font-weight:700` instead of `bold`) or return the element’s outer HTML if you prefer a string result.

Happy hacking, and stay secure! 🚀

