## 7.  Common Security Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them

| Pitfall | What’s at risk | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Hard‑coded secrets** | Leaks to GitHub, code reviews, etc. | Use environment variables or secrets manager. |
| **Public Swagger UI** | Exposes endpoint list, parameter names, example payloads. | Disable in prod, secure behind auth or IP whitelist. |
| **No rate limiting** | Brute‑force, credential stuffing. | Apply global rate limiter + per‑route limits. |
| **No input validation** | SQL/NoSQL injection, XSS. | Validate all inputs with Joi/ajv. |
| **Returning stack traces** | Information disclosure. | Only log internally; send generic error messages to clients. |
| **Missing CORS headers** | CSRF or cross‑origin abuse. | Use `cors` middleware with whitelist. |
| **Using weak JWT algorithms** | Token tampering. | Use `RS256` or `ES256`, disable `none` algorithm. |

