**Short answer** – Yes.  You can keep the `<img>` in the string and wrap everything that you want to hide in a container that is hidden by default and only made visible on `:hover` (or with a simple mouse‑over/out event).  
Below is a complete, safe, and reusable pattern you can drop into your project.

---

## 1. Separate the two parts of the content

```js
const stationInfo = {
  [strand]: {
    image: '<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/.../ef1f442c-24bf-40c8-a82b-e89b71c66ecf_3000.jpg?v=1704458402437" onclick="this.requestFullscreen()" class="center" alt="View of the Grand Canal" width="400">',
    text: `
      View of the Grand Canal: Santa Maria della Salute and the Dogana from Campo Santa Maria Zobenigo (c1743) by Bernardo Bellotto.
      <br><br>
      From the <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RJP#full-artwork-details">Getty's Collection Online</a>.
    `
  },

  [down]: {
    image: '<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/.../ef390b6c-6021-4738-92fe-2338c38cc2af_3000.jpg?v=1704459300215" onclick="this.requestFullscreen()" class="center" alt="A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité" width="400">',
    text: `
      A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité (c1763) by Jean‑Baptiste Raguenet.
      <br><br>
      From the <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RBA">Getty's Collection Online</a>.
    `
  }
};
```

*Why this matters*:  
- You no longer mix plain text with markup in a single string, so you can target the text independently.  
- If `stationInfo` is ever filled from user input, you can safely sanitize the *text* part only.

---

## 2. Render the image immediately; hide the text

```js
function displayStationInfo(id) {
  const container = document.getElementById('ghostinfo');
  const info = stationInfo[id];

  // keep only the image in the container – the text is stored in a hidden <span>
  container.innerHTML = `
    ${info.image}
    <span class="station-text hidden">${info.text}</span>
  `;
}
```

*Explanation*:  
- `hidden` is a CSS class that sets `display:none;`.  
- The `<span>` holds all the explanatory text.  
- Because it is inside the same container (`#ghostinfo`), it will be affected by the `:hover` rule that follows.

---

## 3. Show the hidden text on mouse‑over

```css
/* 1. Hide the text by default  */
.station-text.hidden {
  display: none;
}

/* 2. Reveal the text when the user hovers over the container  */
#ghostinfo:hover .station-text.hidden {
  display: inline;          /* or block – whatever layout you want */
  color: #333;              /* optional styling */
  background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
  padding: 0.3em 0.5em;
  border-radius: 4px;
}

/* 3. (Optional) Give the container a subtle hint that it’s interactive */
#ghostinfo:hover {
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

*Why CSS?*  
- Pure CSS is the most efficient way: no JS events, no re‑rendering, and it works in all browsers.  
- The effect is completely independent of the rest of your code.

---

## 4. Putting it together

```js
// Somewhere in your game logic – e.g. when a round starts
function startRound(referencePoint) {
  // …
  displayStationInfo(referencePoint);   // shows only the image

  // … rest of your logic
}
```

---

## 5. Security note: sanitisation

If any part of `stationInfo` can be controlled by a user (or an external source), you **must** sanitize it before inserting into the DOM.  A safe approach is to:

```js
// Using DOMPurify (https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify)
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

function safeRender(id) {
  const container = document.getElementById('ghostinfo');
  const info = stationInfo[id];

  const cleanImage = DOMPurify.sanitize(info.image);
  const cleanText  = DOMPurify.sanitize(info.text, {ALLOWED_TAGS: ['br','a']});

  container.innerHTML = `
    ${cleanImage}
    <span class="station-text hidden">${cleanText}</span>
  `;
}
```

`DOMPurify` will strip any malicious `<script>` tags, attributes, etc., while leaving the allowed tags you specify.

---

## 6. Optional – a JavaScript fallback

If you prefer or need to support very old browsers that don’t honour CSS `:hover` on non‑link elements, you can attach a small JS event listener:

```js
const ghostinfo = document.getElementById('ghostinfo');

ghostinfo.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
  ghostinfo.querySelector('.station-text.hidden').classList.remove('hidden');
});

ghostinfo.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
  ghostinfo.querySelector('.station-text.hidden').classList.add('hidden');
});
```

But the CSS solution is cleaner and preferred.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Store image and text separately** in `stationInfo`.  
2. Render only the image into `#ghostinfo`.  
3. Wrap the text in a hidden `<span>`.  
4. Use CSS `#ghostinfo:hover .station-text {display:inline;}` to reveal it.  
5. Sanitize any user‑provided content with a library like DOMPurify.

That gives you a safe, efficient, and user‑friendly way to show the image immediately while hiding the descriptive text until the user hovers over the `#ghostinfo` div.

