## 2. PowerShell Client (chat-client.ps1)

The PowerShell script uses the **`WebSocket`** class in .NET (`System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket`) to connect to the server, send messages, and listen for incoming ones.

```powershell
# chat-client.ps1
# ====================
# A simple WebSocket chat client for PowerShell 5.0+
# ====================

param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory = $true)]
    [string]$ServerUri = 'ws://localhost:3000/socket.io/?EIO=4&transport=websocket',

    [Parameter(Mandatory = $false)]
    [string]$Name = 'ps-client'
)

# Helper: encode a Socket.IO packet (type, payload)
function Encode-IOPacket {
    param([string]$type, $payload)
    # Socket.IO binary packet format: <type><payload>
    # For 'chat' events we use type=4
    # For 'connect' we use type=2
    $payloadBytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($payload)
    $packet = @([byte]$type) + $payloadBytes
    return $packet
}

# Helper: decode a Socket.IO packet (returns type, payload)
function Decode-IOPacket {
    param([byte[]]$bytes)
    if ($bytes.Length -eq 0) { return @('unknown', '') }
    $type = $bytes[0]
    $payload = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString($bytes[1..($bytes.Length-1)])
    return @($type, $payload)
}

# Establish WebSocket connection
$ws = [System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket]::new()
Write-Host "Connecting to $ServerUri ..."
$uri = [System.Uri]$ServerUri
$ws.ConnectAsync($uri, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Wait()

Write-Host "Connection opened. Press Ctrl+C to exit." -ForegroundColor Green

# Send an initial "connect" packet (type 2) with client ID
$initPacket = Encode-IOPacket -type 2 -payload ""
$ws.SendAsync([ArraySegment[byte]]$initPacket, [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketMessageType]::Binary, $true, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Wait()

# Background job to receive messages
$receiveJob = Start-Job -ScriptBlock {
    param($ws)
    while ($ws.State -eq 'Open') {
        $buffer = New-Object byte[] 4096
        $result = $ws.ReceiveAsync([ArraySegment[byte]]$buffer, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Wait()
        $count = $ws.ReceiveAsync([ArraySegment[byte]]$buffer, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Result.Count
        if ($count -gt 0) {
            $packet = Decode-IOPacket -bytes $buffer[0..($count-1)]
            $type = $packet[0]
            $payload = $packet[1]
            if ($type -eq 4) { # chat event
                # Socket.IO event data is JSON: ["event", {"data": ...}]
                $data = ConvertFrom-Json $payload
                Write-Host "`n[Chat] $($data[1].id): $($data[1].text)" -ForegroundColor Cyan
            }
        }
    }
} -ArgumentList $ws

# Main loop: read user input and send chat messages
try {
    while ($true) {
        $line = Read-Host -Prompt "$Name> "
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($line)) { continue }
        # Build Socket.IO packet for event 'chat' (type 4) with JSON payload
        $event = 'chat'
        $jsonPayload = ConvertTo-Json @{id=$Name;text=$line;ts=(Get-Date).ToString('o')} -Compress
        $packet = Encode-IOPacket -type 4 -payload $event,$jsonPayload
        $ws.SendAsync([ArraySegment[byte]]$packet, [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketMessageType]::Binary, $true, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Wait()
    }
} catch {
    Write-Warning "Connection lost: $_"
} finally {
    $ws.CloseAsync([System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketCloseStatus]::NormalClosure, "Bye", [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).Wait()
    Stop-Job $receiveJob | Out-Null
    Remove-Job $receiveJob | Out-Null
}
```

**How it works**

1. The script connects to the Socket.IO endpoint (`/socket.io/?...`) with WebSocket.
2. It sends a **connect** packet (`type 2`).
3. It listens for incoming packets on a background job.
4. When the user types a line, it sends a **chat** packet (`type 4`) that contains a JSON array of the event name and the message payload.

> **Note** – Socket.IO’s wire protocol is a bit quirky; this script implements only the bare essentials for a “chat” event. If you add namespaces or authentication, you’ll need to extend the packet builder.

