**Short answer**  
Yes – you can create a “burn‑to‑reveal” effect purely with CSS.  
Below is a minimal, fully‑stand‑alone example that you can drop into an HTML file and tweak to your taste.  
I’ll walk you through the trick, then finish with a quick note about the security angles a researcher might care about.

---

## 1. The effect in a nutshell

The trick relies on:

| Technique | Why it works |
|-----------|--------------|
| **`text-fill-color: transparent` + `text-stroke`** | Makes the text itself invisible so we can paint on it with a gradient or mask. |
| **`mask-image`** or **`clip-path`** | Carves the shape of the text out of a moving “fire” pattern. |
| **Keyframes that change `background-position` and `transform`** | Drives the illusion of flames crawling over the letters. |
| **`filter: drop-shadow` / `text-shadow`** | Adds the “glow” that a real fire would have. |

---

## 2. Demo Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Burn‑to‑Reveal Text</title>
<style>
  body { 
    background:#111; 
    display:flex; 
    align-items:center; 
    justify-content:center; 
    height:100vh; 
    margin:0;
  }

  .burn-text {
    font-size: 8rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    font-family: 'Montserrat', sans-serif;
    color: #fff;
    position: relative;
    /* Make the real text invisible */
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    text-fill-color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-stroke: 1px #000;
    text-stroke: 1px #000;

    /* Fire pattern mask */
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      120deg,
      rgba(255, 70, 0, .9),
      rgba(255, 200, 0, .8),
      rgba(255, 70, 0, .9)
    );
    background-size: 200% 200%;
    background-repeat: repeat;
    -webkit-background-clip: text; /* Safari */
    background-clip: text;

    /* Animate the fire */
    animation: burn 3s linear infinite;
    /* Add a glow */
    text-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(255,70,0,.7);
  }

  @keyframes burn {
    0%   { background-position: 0% 50%; }
    50%  { background-position: 100% 50%; }
    100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
  }

  /* Optional: a flickering overlay */
  .burn-text::after {
    content:"";
    position:absolute;
    inset:0;
    background:radial-gradient(circle, transparent 60%, rgba(255, 255, 255, .1) 100%);
    animation:flicker 0.15s infinite linear;
    mix-blend-mode: lighten;
  }

  @keyframes flicker {
    0%, 100% { opacity:.3; }
    50% { opacity:.6; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="burn-text">🔥 FIRE</div>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

1. **`background-clip: text`** turns the text into a mask for the background gradient, so the gradient only shows through the glyphs.
2. The gradient is a *dynamic* “fire” color blend that moves horizontally in `@keyframes burn`.
3. The `::after` pseudo‑element adds a quick flicker by overlaying a semi‑transparent white radial gradient that lightens the text in a jittery way.
4. `text-shadow` and the gradient itself produce the glow.

Feel free to:

- Swap the gradient colors for orange‑red blends.
- Change `font-size` or `animation-duration`.
- Add `transform: rotate(1deg)` to each keyframe to make the flames look more “alive”.

---

## 3. Security‑researcher checklist

1. **Avoid user‑generated inline styles**  
   If you let the end‑user set the gradient or animation‑duration via JavaScript, you open yourself up to XSS.  
   *Mitigation*: whitelist a small set of safe values and sanitize anything coming from outside the DOM.

2. **Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP)**  
   If you’re embedding the animation in a site that uses CSP, remember that `style-src` must permit inline styles or use a hash/nonce.  
   *Mitigation*: Move the CSS into an external stylesheet or use a nonce for inline `<style>` blocks.

3. **SVG or Canvas injection**  
   Some fire‑effects use an SVG filter or a `<canvas>` canvas.  
   *Mitigation*: Validate all assets, disable `sandbox` if you’re not using it, and never let untrusted data reach the rendering engine without sanitization.

4. **Performance & DoS**  
   A heavy `filter` or large text with many shadows can cause GPU spikes.  
   *Mitigation*: Benchmark on target devices and consider disabling the effect on low‑end hardware.

5. **Accessibility**  
   Ensure that the fire animation does not trigger seizures or cause motion sickness.  
   *Mitigation*: Provide a user toggle to disable animations or respect the OS “Reduce motion” preference (`@media (prefers-reduced-motion)`).

---

### TL;DR

- Use `background-clip: text` + a moving gradient + a flicker overlay to simulate flames.  
- The provided snippet is a ready‑to‑paste solution.  
- From a security point of view, treat any dynamic styling as a potential XSS vector and keep CSP tight.

Happy coding – and fire up that text! 🔥

