Below is a “security‑researcher‑friendly” walkthrough for adding **column‑level filtering** and **global search** to a data grid.  
I’ll cover:

| What you’re building | Why it matters for a security researcher |
|----------------------|------------------------------------------|
| **Grid** | Visualise logs, network events, threat intel, or any tabular data you want to triage. |
| **Column filters** | Drill‑down by protocol, IP, severity, etc. |
| **Global search** | Quickly locate a keyword or identifier across the whole table. |
| **Security** | Make sure the filtering doesn’t expose sensitive data or open injection / XSS holes. |

I’ll use **DataTables** (jQuery) in the example because it’s one of the most popular client‑side grids, and it supports *both* column filters and global search out‑of‑the‑box. I’ll also give you a minimal **server‑side** implementation in **PHP** (but the same pattern works in Node, Python, etc.).  

Feel free to swap the tech stack (ag‑Grid, React‑Table, Handsontable, etc.) – the concepts are identical.

---

## 1. Client‑Side Grid – DataTables

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Security Grid – Column + Global Filter</title>
  <!-- DataTables CSS -->
  <link rel="stylesheet"
        href="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.13.7/css/jquery.dataTables.min.css">
  <!-- jQuery -->
  <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.0.min.js"></script>
  <!-- DataTables JS -->
  <script src="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.13.7/js/jquery.dataTables.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    /* Optional styling for column filter inputs */
    tfoot input {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 3px;
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<table id="security-table" class="display" style="width:100%">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Timestamp</th>
      <th>Source IP</th>
      <th>Destination IP</th>
      <th>Protocol</th>
      <th>Severity</th>
      <th>Event</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>

  <!-- Optional: place column‑filter inputs in <tfoot> -->
  <tfoot>
    <tr>
      <th>Timestamp</th>
      <th>Source IP</th>
      <th>Destination IP</th>
      <th>Protocol</th>
      <th>Severity</th>
      <th>Event</th>
    </tr>
  </tfoot>
</table>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  // 1️⃣ Create the DataTable instance
  var table = $('#security-table').DataTable({
    // 2️⃣ Load data from a server (server‑side processing)
    ajax: {
      url: '/api/events',
      type: 'POST',
      dataSrc: 'data',   // JSON field that contains the array
      // Optionally, send CSRF token or auth header here
    },
    // 3️⃣ Tell DataTables we want to do filtering on the server
    serverSide: true,
    processing: true,

    // 4️⃣ Columns definition – helps with type‑aware sorting/filtering
    columns: [
      { data: 'timestamp' },
      { data: 'src_ip' },
      { data: 'dst_ip' },
      { data: 'protocol' },
      { data: 'severity' },
      { data: 'event' }
    ],

    // 5️⃣ Optional: Enable built‑in search box
    dom: 'lrtip', // remove default search box if you prefer custom

    // 6️⃣ Enable per‑column filtering via the footer
    initComplete: function () {
      this.api().columns().every(function () {
        var column = this;
        // Create an input element for each column header
        var input = $('<input type="text" placeholder="Search '+column.footer().textContent+'" />')
          .appendTo($(column.footer()).empty())
          .on('keyup change clear', function () {
            if (column.search() !== this.value) {
              column.search(this.value).draw();
            }
          });
      });
    }
  });

  // 7️⃣ Add a global search input somewhere in the UI
  $('<input type="text" id="global-search" placeholder="Global Search…">')
    .insertBefore('#security-table')
    .on('keyup change clear', function () {
      table.search(this.value).draw();
    });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What this does

1. **DataTables** pulls data from `/api/events` using AJAX.  
2. **Server‑side** (`serverSide: true`) means that all filtering, pagination, and sorting happen on the server. The client just sends the current search values.  
3. Column filters live in the `<tfoot>` (you can also put them in `<thead>` or outside the table). Each column’s filter only affects that column.  
4. Global search (`#global-search`) runs across *all* columns.

---

## 2. Server‑Side Endpoint – PHP Example

```php
<?php
// api/events.php
header('Content-Type: application/json');

// 1️⃣ Connect to your DB securely
$pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=sec_db;charset=utf8mb4',
               'user', 'pass',
               [PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION]);

// 2️⃣ Read DataTables parameters (DataTables sends a lot)
$draw      = $_POST['draw'] ?? 0;
$start     = intval($_POST['start'] ?? 0);
$length    = intval($_POST['length'] ?? 10);

// 3️⃣ Global search term (escaped by PDO)
$searchTerm = $_POST['search']['value'] ?? '';
$globalWhere = '';
$globalParams = [];
if ($searchTerm !== '') {
    $globalWhere = "WHERE (timestamp LIKE :gt OR src_ip LIKE :gt OR dst_ip LIKE :gt OR protocol LIKE :gt OR severity LIKE :gt OR event LIKE :gt)";
    $globalParams[':gt'] = '%'.$searchTerm.'%';
}

// 4️⃣ Per‑column filters (if you enabled them in the client)
$columnWhere = [];
$columnParams = [];
$columns = ['timestamp','src_ip','dst_ip','protocol','severity','event'];
foreach ($columns as $idx => $col) {
    $value = $_POST["columns[$idx][search][value]"] ?? '';
    if ($value !== '') {
        $param = ":col_$idx";
        $columnWhere[] = "$col LIKE $param";
        $columnParams[$param] = "%$value%";
    }
}
$combinedWhere = '';
$combinedParams = array_merge($globalParams, $columnParams);
if ($globalWhere && $columnWhere) {
    $combinedWhere = "($globalWhere) AND (" . implode(' AND ', $columnWhere) . ')';
} elseif ($globalWhere) {
    $combinedWhere = $globalWhere;
} elseif ($columnWhere) {
    $combinedWhere = implode(' AND ', $columnWhere);
}

// 5️⃣ Order by (DataTables sends column index and direction)
$orderClause = '';
$orderColIndex = intval($_POST['order'][0]['column'] ?? 0);
$orderDir = ($_POST['order'][0]['dir'] ?? 'asc') === 'desc' ? 'DESC' : 'ASC';
if (isset($columns[$orderColIndex])) {
    $orderClause = "ORDER BY {$columns[$orderColIndex]} $orderDir";
}

// 6️⃣ Total records (without filtering)
$totalStmt = $pdo->query('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events');
$totalRecords = $totalStmt->fetchColumn();

// 7️⃣ Total filtered (after applying filters)
$filteredStmt = $pdo->prepare("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events $combinedWhere");
$filteredStmt->execute($combinedParams);
$filteredRecords = $filteredStmt->fetchColumn();

// 8️⃣ Fetch data page
$dataStmt = $pdo->prepare("
    SELECT timestamp, src_ip, dst_ip, protocol, severity, event
    FROM events
    $combinedWhere
    $orderClause
    LIMIT :start, :length
");
$dataStmt->bindValue(':start', $start, PDO::PARAM_INT);
$dataStmt->bindValue(':length', $length, PDO::PARAM_INT);
foreach ($combinedParams as $k => $v) {
    $dataStmt->bindValue($k, $v, PDO::PARAM_STR);
}
$dataStmt->execute();
$rows = $dataStmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

// 9️⃣ Build the JSON payload expected by DataTables
$response = [
    'draw'            => intval($draw),
    'recordsTotal'    => $totalRecords,
    'recordsFiltered' => $filteredRecords,
    'data'            => $rows
];

echo json_encode($response);
```

### Security Checklist

| Check | Why | What to do |
|-------|-----|------------|
| **Prepared statements** | Prevents SQL injection. | Use PDO/parameterized queries like above. |
| **Validate & sanitize** | Ensure inputs are of expected type (e.g., numeric `start`, `length`). | Cast to `int`, check ranges. |
| **CSRF protection** | DataTables AJAX can be abused. | Include a CSRF token (via header or hidden form field) and validate on the server. |
| **Authorization** | Only authenticated analysts should see data. | Use session auth, JWT, or OAuth, and check permissions before returning rows. |
| **Rate limiting** | Prevent abuse or DoS via rapid filtering. | Throttle requests per IP/user. |
| **Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP)** | Mitigates XSS if you allow user input to appear in the table. | Set a strict CSP that allows only trusted sources. |
| **Escape output** | Prevent data‑leakage or XSS when rendering table cells. | DataTables escapes by default; if you use `render` functions, always escape or use `textContent`. |

---

## 3. Quick Alternatives

| Grid Library | Column Filtering | Global Search | Server‑Side |
|--------------|------------------|---------------|------------|
| **ag‑Grid** (enterprise) | Built‑in column filter panel | Quick filter | `rowModel: 'serverSide'` |
| **React‑Table** | Custom column filters (hooks) | Global filter hook | You implement the API |
| **Handsontable** | Header dropdowns | Search box | Use `hot.getDataAtRowProp` on server |
| **Tabulator** | Header filter widgets | `setFilter('global', …)` | `ajaxFiltering: true` |

> **Tip:** If you’re already using DataTables, you’re good to go. If you’re building a SPA in React/Vue/Angular, consider **react-table** or **ag‑Grid** – they integrate nicely with modern state management.

---

## 4. What a Security Researcher Might Do Next

1. **Add Role‑Based Visibility**  
   Show or hide columns (e.g., raw IP addresses) depending on the analyst’s clearance.

2. **Log Filtering Actions**  
   Store filter configurations as “saved searches” so analysts can re‑apply them or share with teammates.

3. **Export & Anomaly Detection**  
   Provide CSV/JSON exports of the current view, and hook into a rule engine (e.g., Snort, Suricata) for alerts.

4. **Audit**  
   Log every search/filter request with timestamp, user ID, and filter payload. Useful for later forensic analysis.

---

## 5. Summary

* **Client side (DataTables)** gives instant UI feedback.  
* **Server side** is mandatory for security‑sensitive data: no raw queries, no exposed DB schema, no client‑side injection.  
* **Column filters + global search** are simply two types of `WHERE` clauses that you build from the UI inputs.  
* **Security**: always validate, parameterise, and authorise.  

Feel free to ask if you need a snippet for another framework (React‑Table, ag‑Grid, etc.) or if you want to see the same logic in Node/Python. Happy hunting! 🚀

