### 2️⃣ Security Checklist – What the Demo Covers

| Control | How It’s Implemented | Why It Matters |
|---------|----------------------|----------------|
| **Same‑origin policy** | No CORS headers added on the server. | Prevents other sites from reading responses. |
| **CSRF token** | `X-CSRF-Token` header set via meta tag. | Defends POST/GET actions that change state. |
| **HTTP‑Only secure cookies** | `credentials: 'include'` + backend should set `HttpOnly; Secure` cookies. | Protects session tokens from XSS. |
| **XSS protection** | DataTables auto‑escapes, optional DOMPurify. | Stops injection of malicious scripts. |
| **Content Security Policy** | Meta CSP limiting sources. | Blocks inline scripts, only allows trusted CDNs. |
| **Error handling** | Try‑catch + user‑friendly message. | Avoid leaking stack traces. |
| **Rate limiting / throttling** | Not shown in client – must be on server. | Prevents brute‑force or DoS attacks. |
| **HTTPS only** | Assume the page is served via HTTPS. | Keeps data confidential in transit. |

### 3️⃣ Minimal Backend Example (Node‑Express)

> *If you need a quick backend stub to test the front‑end, copy the snippet below.*

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');
const app = express();

app.use(helmet()); // Sets various secure headers
app.use(cookieParser());

// Simple in‑memory CSRF token generator
const csrf = require('csurf');
const csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });

// Serve static files (the HTML above)
app.use(express.static('public'));

// API endpoint
app.get('/api/users', csrfProtection, (req, res) => {
  // Example data – in real life pull from DB
  const users = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Алексей', email: 'alex@example.com', created_at: '2024‑01‑12' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Мария', email: 'maria@example.com', created_at: '2024‑01‑15' }
    // ...
  ];
  res.json(users);
});

// CSRF token endpoint for front‑end (if not using cookie)
app.get('/csrf-token', csrfProtection, (req, res) => {
  res.json({ csrfToken: req.csrfToken() });
});

// Start server
app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on https://localhost:3000'));
```

> **NOTE** – The above server uses **helmet** for secure HTTP headers, `csurf` for CSRF protection, and `express.static` to serve the HTML file. The CSRF token is sent as a cookie; the front‑end can read it via JavaScript or a dedicated endpoint.

