```

---

## 10. Security checklist (quick‑look)

| ✅ | Item | Why it matters |
|----|------|----------------|
| ✅ | **Helmet** | Adds HTTP headers to mitigate common web exploits (XSS, clickjacking, etc.) |
| ✅ | **Rate limiting** | Prevents brute‑force and DoS attacks |
| ✅ | **CORS** | Restricts which origins can call the API (tighten in prod) |
| ✅ | **HTTPS** | Encrypt data in transit – enable behind reverse proxy (NGINX, CloudFront, etc.) |
| ✅ | **Input validation** | Stops malformed payloads & reduces injection risk |
| ✅ | **Unique index on email** | DB‑level safeguard against duplicates |
| ✅ | **Error handling** | No stack traces leaked to clients |
| ✅ | **Environment variables** | Keeps secrets out of code |
| ✅ | **Logging** | Monitor for anomalies (you might add Winston/Morgan in production) |

---

## 11. Extending the API

- **Email confirmation**: After storing, send a confirmation mail via SendGrid/Mailgun.
- **Unsubscribe endpoint**: `DELETE /api/subscribe?email=…`
- **Admin panel**: List all subscribers, export CSV.
- **Testing**: Use Jest/Supertest to cover happy path, validation, rate‑limit, duplicate email, DB errors.

---

> **Security note** – Always keep your MongoDB instance behind a firewall (or VPC) and use authentication. For production, consider enabling TLS on the driver (`tls=true`, `tlsCAFile`, etc.) and rotating credentials.

Happy coding – and stay safe! 🚨

