Below is a **complete “how‑to”** – from the client‑side HTML/JS that asks the user for a name when the game ends, to a tiny server that safely writes the record to a plain‑text file (`toplist.txt`) and returns the updated high‑score table.  
I also point out the security pitfalls and best‑practice mitigations you should keep in mind when you move a game from “pure demo” to a real world deployment.

> **TL;DR**  
> 1. Add an `endGame()` handler that prompts for a name and calls `submitScore(name,score)`  
> 2. `submitScore` does a `POST /scores` via `fetch`.  
> 3. The server (Node.js/Express example) receives the payload, sanitises it, appends a line to `toplist.txt`, then re‑reads the file, sorts, and sends back the top‑10 list.  
> 4. The client renders the list in a modal or a dedicated `<div>`.  

---

## 1.  Client side – Trigger the “Top‑list” flow

Add a small helper after the `endGame()` function:

```html
<script>
/* ... existing code ... */

/* Called from endGame() */
function submitScore(name, score) {
  // Basic validation on the client side – you *must* still validate on the server
  name = name.trim();
  if (!name || name.length > 20) {
    alert('Please enter a valid name (max 20 chars).');
    return;
  }
  if (!Number.isInteger(score) || score < 0) {
    alert('Score must be a non‑negative integer.');
    return;
  }

  /* Send the record to the server */
  fetch('/scores', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ name, score })
  })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(data => {
      // Render the updated top‑list
      renderTopList(data.top);
      // Show a nice modal or toast
      showModal('Thanks, ' + name + '! Your score of ' + score + ' has been recorded.');
    })
    .catch(err => console.error(err));
}

/* Render the table in a <div id="top-list"> */
function renderTopList(top) {
  const container = document.getElementById('top-list');
  const rows = top.map((r, idx) =>
    `<tr><td>${idx+1}</td><td>${r.name}</td><td>${r.score}</td></tr>`
  ).join('');
  container.innerHTML = `<h3>Top 10 Scores</h3><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Name</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows}</tbody></table>`;
}

/* Helper to show a simple modal (or you can use a library) */
function showModal(msg) {
  const modal = document.getElementById('modal');
  modal.textContent = msg;
  modal.style.display = 'block';
}
</script>
```

Add the two new elements somewhere in the `<body>`:

