Below is a **complete, end‑to‑end example** that you can drop into your org and use as a “List Action” (i.e. a button that shows up on the “List” view of any object).  
When the user clicks the button they see a modal popup in which they can select a file to upload.  
Once the file is uploaded we programmatically create a **ContentDocumentLink** for each record that was selected in the list view, so the same file appears as an attachment for all those records.

> **Assumptions**  
> * You’re using **Salesforce Lightning Experience**.  
> * The user has **Create** access on the object and on **ContentDocumentLink** (this is automatic for most orgs).  
> * You have the **Lightning App Builder** and **Salesforce CLI** available.  
> * You’re comfortable adding an Apex class and an LWC.

---

## 1. Apex controller – `AttachmentBulkUploader.cls`

This Apex method receives an array of record Ids and a single **ContentVersion** Id (the file that the user just uploaded).  
It loops through the Ids, creates a **ContentDocumentLink** for each, and returns a success message.

```apex
public with sharing class AttachmentBulkUploader {
    @AuraEnabled
    public static String attachToRecords(List<Id> recordIds, Id contentVersionId) {
        if (recordIds == null || recordIds.isEmpty() || contentVersionId == null) {
            throw new AuraException('Invalid input');
        }

        // Get the ContentDocument Id for the uploaded ContentVersion
        Id contentDocId = [SELECT ContentDocumentId FROM ContentVersion
                           WHERE Id = :contentVersionId LIMIT 1].ContentDocumentId;

        List<ContentDocumentLink> links = new List<ContentDocumentLink>();
        for (Id recId : recordIds) {
            links.add(new ContentDocumentLink(
                ContentDocumentId = contentDocId,
                LinkedEntityId = recId,
                ShareType = 'V',           // Viewer – change to 'I' if you need Editor
                Visibility = 'AllUsers'
            ));
        }
        insert links;

        return 'File attached to ' + recordIds.size() + ' records.';
    }
}
```

> **Why `ContentDocumentLink`?**  
> It’s the modern way to attach files in Lightning. `Attachment` objects are legacy and have a “maximum file size 10 MB” limitation in Lightning.

---

## 2. Lightning Web Component – `bulkAttachModal`

The LWC is used as a **List Action**.  
It renders a modal with a **file‑upload** element, and when the user clicks **Attach**, it calls the Apex method.

### a. Component metadata – `bulkAttachModal.html`

```html
