## 1.  Security‑first overview of the assignment

| Threat | What it can do in this context | Mitigation (what to do in the code / project) |
|--------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|
| **Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)** | The JSON file comes from a server and is rendered into the DOM.  If an attacker injects `<script>` or HTML tags in the JSON values, the browser will execute them when we `innerHTML` the data. | • Never use `innerHTML` with data that has not been sanitized. <br>• Use `textContent` or create DOM elements with `document.createElement`. <br>• If you absolutely must use HTML, run the content through a sanitizer (DOMPurify, etc.). |
| **Cross‑Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)** | If the JSON server is on a different origin, the browser will block the request unless the server allows it. | • Make sure the API sends the correct `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header (or use a proxy). <br>• In dev, you can set `mode: "cors"` in `ajax.getJSON`. |
| **Man‑in‑the‑Middle (MITM)** | A network attacker could tamper with the JSON data if it is fetched over plain HTTP. | • Always fetch over HTTPS. <br>• In production, enforce the `Secure` flag on cookies, HSTS, etc. |
| **Content‑Security‑Policy (CSP) violations** | If you load RxJS or other libraries from a CDN, you open the door to an attacker injecting a malicious CDN. | • Serve RxJS locally (or use a subresource integrity hash). <br>• Set a CSP header that whitelists only the domains you trust. |
| **Injection in URLs** | If the URL for the JSON file is built from user input, an attacker could trick the app into fetching an arbitrary resource. | • Validate / whitelist the URL, or keep it hard‑coded. |
| **CSRF** | Not relevant here because we are not performing a state‑changing request on the server – only GET. | — |

### Quick checklist before you ship

1. **Serve RxJS locally** (or use a CDN with SRI).  
2. **Use CSP** to restrict script, style, image, font, and connect origins.  
3. **Sanitize any data** before inserting into the DOM.  
4. **Validate the JSON source URL** (no user‑supplied strings).  
5. **Use HTTPS** for all network calls.  
6. **Error handling** – log or report failures without leaking sensitive details.  

---

## 2.  Minimal implementation (index.html + index.js)

Below is a **self‑contained** example that demonstrates:

* A single‑page UI with two buttons – “Show data” and “Delete”.
* RxJS (`fromEvent`, `ajax.getJSON`, `map`, `take`, `tap`) wired up via the `rxjs` library.
* Safe DOM updates – we never use `innerHTML` with untrusted data.
* Simple, in‑memory state (array of rows) to keep track of the last added row.

> **Tip** – keep the RxJS bundle **local**.  
> Place `rxjs.umd.min.js` in the same folder (download from <https://cdnjs.com/libraries/rxjs>).

### index.html

