You’re asking *why the earliest items appear last in the visual stack*.  
By default, the order that `for‑loop` creates elements is the same order they appear in the DOM. In CSS, later elements are painted on top of earlier ones (unless `z-index` changes that). So if the list is rendered *chronologically* (oldest → newest), the **newest** items will sit on top. That is the opposite of “earliest last” that you expect if you want the oldest to dominate the view.

