Below is a **security‑first** take‑away followed by the concrete JavaScript you can drop into your page so that users can jump back to the previous question.

---

## 1. Quick Security Checklist (for the snippet you posted)

| Issue | What it means | Fix / Mitigation |
|-------|---------------|------------------|
| **Inline `onclick` handlers** | Inline handlers expose the DOM to XSS and make unit‑testing harder. | Use `addEventListener` instead. |
| **Unescaped user‑supplied content** | `quizData` and `option` strings are injected straight into `innerHTML`. If any of those come from an untrusted source you can run arbitrary JS. | Sanitize or escape before rendering (e.g. `textContent` or a library). |
| **Unescaped option in `onclick` attribute** | The string interpolation of `${option}` in `<div onclick="checkAnswer('${option}')">…</div>` can break out of the attribute if `option` contains a quote or HTML. | Escape quotes or, better, attach listeners via JS. |
| **Missing CSRF protection** | The POST request to `sunucu.php` is sent without a token. | Add a CSRF token that the server validates. |
| **No bounds checking on index** | `KullanilanQuestion` can become negative or exceed the array length. | Guard against under‑/over‑flows. |
| **Potential replay / tampering** | The score is calculated purely client‑side. A user can tamper with it before posting. | Validate score server‑side or recalc from logged answers. |
| **No HTTPS mention** | Ensure the request is sent over HTTPS to protect the full name, score, etc. | Use `https://…` or force HTTPS. |

---

## 2. Adding a “Back” button that is safe

Below is a **self‑contained** function that

1. Moves the quiz back one question.
2. Correctly adjusts `soruSira`, `score`, and the answer array.
3. Guards against going before the first question.
4. Uses `addEventListener` instead of inline HTML attributes.

```html
<!-- Somewhere in your markup -->
<button id="backBtn" class="nav-btn">◀ Back</button>
```

```javascript
/* ----------  Back‑Navigation Code  ---------- */

/**
 * Safely go back to the previous question.
 * If we are at the very first question nothing happens.
 */
function goBack() {
  if (KullanilanQuestion <= 0) {
    // already on first question – nothing to do
    console.warn('Back requested at first question – ignoring.');
    return;
  }

  // 1. Remove the last answer record
  const lastAnswer = KullaniciSoruCevaplari.pop();

  // 2. Adjust the score if the removed answer was correct
  if (
    lastAnswer &&
    lastAnswer.KullaniciCevabi === quizData[KullanilanQuestion - 1].dogruCevap
  ) {
    score = Math.max(0, score - 1);
  }

  // 3. Update indices
  KullanilanQuestion--;          // point to the previous question
  soruSira--;                    // keep the visible counter in sync
  durum = 'soru';                // force the next render to be a question

  // 4. Show the previous question
  showQuestion();
}

/**
 * Attach the handler to the Back button after the DOM is ready.
 * (If you initialise everything in a `<script defer>` you can
 *   call this straight away; otherwise use DOMContentLoaded.)
 */
document.getElementById('backBtn').addEventListener('click', goBack);
```

### Why this works safely

| Step | What it prevents |
|------|------------------|
| **Index bounds check** (`KullanilanQuestion <= 0`) | Stops underflow. |
| **Answer stack pop** | Keeps the state in sync with the UI. |
| **Score adjustment** | Guarantees the score cannot be higher than the number of answered questions. |
| **`durum = 'soru'`** | Forces the rendering logic to show the question part, bypassing the “konu” (topic) screen that might otherwise be displayed. |
| **`addEventListener`** | No inline handlers → no accidental XSS. |
| **No raw string interpolation** | The back button itself never injects any user data. |

---

## 3. Quick refactor tip: remove inline `onclick`

Instead of:

```html
<div class="option" onclick="checkAnswer('${option}')">${option}</div>
```

Do:

```javascript
const optionDiv = document.createElement('div');
optionDiv.className = 'option';
optionDiv.textContent = option;          // safe – no HTML injection
optionDiv.dataset.answer = option;      // store the answer in a data attribute
optionDiv.addEventListener('click', function () {
  checkAnswer(this.dataset.answer);
});
```

Then change `showQuestion()` accordingly:

```javascript
optionsHtml = '';   // keep using this if you still want innerHTML for other parts
quizData[KullanilanQuestion].options.forEach((option) => {
  const optDiv = document.createElement('div');
  optDiv.className = 'option';
  optDiv.textContent = option;
  optDiv.dataset.answer = option;
  optDiv.addEventListener('click', () => checkAnswer(option));
  questionElement.appendChild(optDiv); // or build a fragment if you prefer
});
```

---

## 4. Final notes

* **Validate on the server.** Even if you keep the back‑navigation logic client‑side, never trust the posted `score` or the array of answers. Recalculate on the server or store the raw answers and compute the score there.
* **HTTPS & CSRF.** Wrap the AJAX call in a `fetch()` that sends a CSRF token and always uses HTTPS.
* **Sanitisation.** If any part of `quizData` comes from a CMS or external source, escape it before inserting into the DOM.

With the `goBack()` routine above and the minor refactor to remove inline handlers, your quiz will be both **user‑friendly** and **harder to abuse**. Happy hacking!

