## Security‑Focused Form‑Handling Case Study  
> **Scenario** – A lightweight “User Interests” registration page  
> **Goal** – Build a fully‑functional PHP form that demonstrates:  
> 1️⃣ Conditionals & Operators for validation  
> 2️⃣ Form‑data sanitisation & validation  
> 3️⃣ Manual submission of form values (e.g. via `cURL`)  
> 4️⃣ Handling form‑arrays (checkboxes, multi‑select)  
> 5️⃣ Processing with `for` / `while` loops  
> 6️⃣ Creating a simple form in PHP  
> 7️⃣ Receiving data safely on the server  
>  
> All with a **security‑researcher’s eye**: guard against XSS, CSRF, SQL injection, and ensure best practices.

---

### 1️⃣  The Form – `register.php`

```php
<?php
/* -------------------------------------------------------
   1.  Start a secure session and generate a CSRF token
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
session_start();
if (empty($_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {
    $_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));
}
$csrf_token = $_SESSION['csrf_token'];
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>User Registration – Interests</title>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Register</h1>

<form action="process.php" method="post">

    <!-- 1️⃣  Hidden CSRF token -->
    <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="<?=htmlspecialchars($csrf_token)?>">

    <!-- 2️⃣  Username (required) -->
    <label>
        Username:
        <input type="text" name="username" required>
    </label>
    <br>

    <!-- 3️⃣  Email (required) -->
    <label>
        Email:
        <input type="email" name="email" required>
    </label>
    <br>

    <!-- 4️⃣  Password (required) -->
    <label>
        Password:
        <input type="password" name="password" required minlength="8">
    </label>
    <br>

    <!-- 5️⃣  Interests (array input) – checkboxes -->
    <fieldset>
        <legend>Select your interests:</legend>
        <?php
        $interests = ['Sports', 'Music', 'Technology', 'Travel', 'Cooking'];
        foreach ($interests as $interest) {
            echo '<label><input type="checkbox" name="interests[]" value="'.htmlspecialchars($interest).'">'.htmlspecialchars($interest).'</label><br>';
        }
        ?>
    </fieldset>

    <button type="submit">Register</button>
</form>
</body>
</html>
```

#### What We’re Doing

| Feature | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------|
| `session_start()` & `csrf_token` | Prevent CSRF by matching the token on submit. |
| `htmlspecialchars()` | Escape output to avoid XSS. |
| `required`, `minlength` | Browser‑side validation; still double‑checked on server. |
| `interests[]` | Demonstrates array inputs (checkboxes). |

---

### 2️⃣  Manual Submission – `send_manual.php`

Sometimes you want to simulate a form post (e.g., automated tests). Here’s how you can send values **manually** using cURL:

```php
<?php
$ch = curl_init('http://localhost/process.php');

$form = [
    'csrf_token' => $_SESSION['csrf_token'], // or any known good token
    'username'   => 'alice',
    'email'      => 'alice@example.com',
    'password'   => 'sEcUr3P@ss!',
    'interests'  => ['Music', 'Travel']     // array input
];

// Build query string
$postfields = [];
foreach ($form as $key => $val) {
    if (is_array($val)) {
        foreach ($val as $v) {
            $postfields[] = rawurlencode($key.'[]').'='.rawurlencode($v);
        }
    } else {
        $postfields[] = rawurlencode($key).'='.rawurlencode($val);
    }
}
$postString = implode('&', $postfields);

curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POST, true);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $postString);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);

$response = curl_exec($ch);
curl_close($ch);

echo $response;
```

> **Security Note** – In real life you’d keep the CSRF token hidden; the above is only for controlled test environments.

---

### 3️⃣  Receiving & Validating – `process.php`

```php
<?php
/* -------------------------------------------------------
   0.  Secure session & CSRF check
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
session_start();

$errors = [];
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] !== 'POST') {
    die('Invalid request method.');
}

// CSRF token validation
if (!isset($_POST['csrf_token']) || !hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'], $_POST['csrf_token'])) {
    die('CSRF validation failed.');
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   1.  Extract & trim values
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
$username  = trim($_POST['username'] ?? '');
$email     = trim($_POST['email'] ?? '');
$password  = $_POST['password'] ?? ''; // don't trim passwords in general
$interests = $_POST['interests'] ?? [];

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   2.  Validate required fields
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
if ($username === '') {
    $errors[] = 'Username is required.';
}
if ($email === '' || !filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {
    $errors[] = 'Valid email is required.';
}
if ($password === '' || strlen($password) < 8) {
    $errors[] = 'Password must be at least 8 characters.';
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   3.  Sanitize interests (array) – prevent XSS & ensure known values
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
$allowedInterests = ['Sports', 'Music', 'Technology', 'Travel', 'Cooking'];
$sanitizedInterests = [];
foreach ($interests as $i) {
    $i = trim($i);
    if (in_array($i, $allowedInterests, true)) {
        $sanitizedInterests[] = $i;          // keep only known interests
    }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   4.  If any errors, display them (escaped)
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
if ($errors) {
    echo '<h3>Errors:</h3><ul>';
    foreach ($errors as $e) {
        echo '<li>'.htmlspecialchars($e).'</li>';
    }
    echo '</ul>';
    exit;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   5.  Hash the password (security!)
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
$pwd_hash = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   6.  Persist to DB (example with PDO + prepared stmt)
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
try {
    $pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=demo;charset=utf8mb4', 'root', '');
    $pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);

    // 6a. Insert user
    $stmt = $pdo->prepare('INSERT INTO users (username, email, pwd_hash) VALUES (:u, :e, :p)');
    $stmt->execute([
        ':u' => $username,
        ':e' => $email,
        ':p' => $pwd_hash
    ]);
    $userId = $pdo->lastInsertId();

    // 6b. Insert interests (many-to-many)
    if ($sanitizedInterests) {
        // prepare a bulk insert
        $ins = $pdo->prepare('INSERT INTO user_interests (user_id, interest) VALUES (:uid, :interest)');
        foreach ($sanitizedInterests as $interest) {
            $ins->execute([':uid' => $userId, ':interest' => $interest]);
        }
    }

    echo '<p>Registration successful!</p>';

} catch (PDOException $e) {
    // Log the error *securely*; never display raw error to users
    error_log($e->getMessage());
    die('Database error. Please try again later.');
}
?>
```

#### Key Security Points

| Step | Security Guard |
|------|----------------|
| CSRF token validation | `hash_equals` prevents timing attacks. |
| `filter_var($email)` | Validates email format. |
| `password_hash` | Secure password storage. |
| Prepared statements | Prevents SQL injection. |
| `htmlspecialchars` | Escapes output (XSS). |
| Sanitise array input | Only known values are stored. |
| Logging | Never expose stack traces to users. |

---

### 4️⃣  Using Loops – Example in `process.php`

We already used a `foreach` loop to sanitize interests.  
Here’s a **while‑loop** example to read the same array in a slightly different way:

```php
/* -------------------------------------------------------
   7.  Process interests with while loop (alternative)
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
$sanitized = [];
$i = 0;
while ($i < count($sanitizedInterests)) {
    $interest = $sanitizedInterests[$i];
    // any extra processing could go here
    $sanitized[] = $interest;
    $i++;
}
```

> **Why loops?**  
> - `foreach` is idiomatic for arrays.  
> - `while` can be used when you need a counter (e.g., for pagination).  

---

### 5️⃣  Summary Checklist

| Topic | How we addressed it |
|-------|---------------------|
| Conditionals & Operators | Multiple `if/elseif` blocks, `empty()`, `strlen()`, `in_array()` |
| Validate Form Data | Email regex, required fields, password length |
| Send Values to a Script Manually | cURL POST example |
| Forms & arrays | Checkbox array (`interests[]`) |
| For & While Loops | `foreach` for sanitisation, `while` for iteration |
| Simple Form in PHP | `register.php` generates HTML with PHP |
| Receive Data from a Form | `process.php` reads `$_POST`, validates, stores |

---

### 6️⃣  Common Pitfalls & How We Avoided Them

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **XSS via unsanitised output** | `htmlspecialchars()` everywhere. |
| **CSRF** | Random token, `hash_equals`. |
| **SQLi** | PDO prepared statements. |
| **Weak passwords** | Enforced minimum length, hashed with `PASSWORD_DEFAULT`. |
| **Unvalidated array data** | Whitelist allowed values. |
| **Information leakage** | `error_log()` for internal errors, generic messages to users. |

---

## Final Thoughts

* The example above demonstrates **how to build a secure form** while touching on all the requested PHP topics.  
* As a security researcher, you’ll often see similar patterns – always validate *both* client‑side *and* server‑side, sanitize inputs, use proper cryptography, and guard against CSRF/XSS.  
* In production, consider adding:

  - Rate limiting / account lock‑out
  - HTTPS enforcement
  - Content Security Policy (CSP)
  - Regular code reviews / static analysis

Feel free to copy, adapt, or extend this example for your own projects!

